[White "Course Introduction"]
[Black "Introduction to the Course"]
[Result "*"]

{ Welcome to Calculation: A Complete Guide for Tournament Players! You must have heard of the adage, “Give a man a fish and you feed him for a day; teach a man to fish and you feed him for a lifetime”. This course offers you both the fish and the tools for fishing. I will show you the most watertight and efficient calculation techniques which are best suited for classical chess games and puzzles. You will also learn to internalise new chess patterns and decode endgame manuals on your own. Ultimately, I want you to improve your learning curve even well after you have completed this course.  The Origin of the Course  Let me share the story of how this course came about. When I first started playing chess competitively some years ago, I realised that the biggest determining factor of chess rating lies in calculation skills. Before long, I found myself devoting most of my time to solving chess puzzles, and soon became obsessed with the drill. At the height of my compulsion, I completed over 300 puzzles in a single day. Other types of chess training such as reading books and playing games took a back seat.  However, even after solving chess puzzles daily for a year, something was not working out. I may have had days where my rating prospered, but it felt undeserved. Even for puzzles solved correctly, I was not ready to bet on my answers before playing them out. When I got a puzzle wrong, I barely spent any time analysing the solution. Instead, I was in a hurry to move on to the next puzzle, hoping to redeem myself from my earlier folly. This resulted in countless streaks of mistakes where I missed even the very elementary tactics. On several occasions, this drove me to utter frustration and I reset my puzzle history just to bury my shame. My poor learning attitude was costly. Despite having solved well over 20,000 puzzles in that year, my improvements failed to reflect my painstaking effort.  This rude awakening propelled me to look for a good calculation system which offers a step-by-step guide to approaching sharp positions. Unfortunately, after exploring many books and videos, I could not find anything of the sort. Sure, I found some fragmented pieces of advice. For example, “Always calculate checks first” and “Loose pieces drop off”. But on their own, they provided little help.   I knew if I wanted to improve, I had to develop my own system. I started keeping a “Puzzle Log” which tracked incorrectly solved puzzles. Each puzzle was labelled with the cause of my mistake such as “poor visualisation” and “incorrect evaluation”. Two weeks into this meticulous record-keeping, I noticed the Puzzle Log was overwhelmed with the label “temporary blindness”. Clearly, my impaired vision was not so temporary. So what was the remedy to blindness? It certainly could not be practice and more practice, given the inordinate amount of time I had already spent on puzzles.  It was time to switch up my approach. I revisited each puzzle I got wrong and analysed it thoroughly with a singular vision in mind. I saw myself giving a chess lecture to an imaginary lower-rated audience, providing hints to solve the puzzle. Trying to become Captain Hindsight has not only sharpened my understanding of old puzzles, but somehow improved my ability to solve new ones. I got better at developing fresh insights into more positions and began compiling them systemically. A skeletal structure of this curriculum on calculation techniques was thus created. In the ensuing years, I personally tested and refined the materials iteratively, eventually teaching to numerous students, many of whom reaped significant rating increases. And now it is my pleasure to share it with you.  Maximise Results  This course opens a new way of viewing the 64 squares, and I want to ensure that you experience the same substantial improvements that my students and I have enjoyed. Hence, I am going to provide three guidelines that will maximise your results.  1. Go through the course sequentially.  I have carefully crafted the course such that each chapter builds on the knowledge covered in previous ones. It is therefore important to go through the course in the order that is presented. Otherwise, you may not understand some of the concepts as deeply as you should.  2. Pay close attention to the informational pages  The informational pages are where I reveal both the execution and the rationale of the techniques. By having a greater understanding of the techniques, you will be able to utilise them more tactfully and efficiently.  3. Execute the techniques conscientiously.  When you see a new puzzle, you may be tempted to slip back to your old techniques. Succumbing to this knee-jerk reaction will be counterproductive. Instead, I urge you to closely rely on what you have learnt from this course, only playing out the moves after checking through all the variations. To facilitate this, I have set the default time for each puzzle to ‘Unlimited’.  Contents  The first chapter introduces The Burger Technique. This is the foundation on which we will build in the subsequent chapters.   The second is on Reciprocal Thinking. It explains how you can swiftly uncover the best candidate move by dredging out all the bad ones.  Following that, in the third chapter, we will learn how to internalise new chess patterns effectively using a four-step guide: Pattern Internalisation Essentials.  The fourth chapter covers Essential Theoretical Endgames. Its main focus is to help you connect the dots in endgame manuals.  Next, in the penultimate chapter, we will deal with important endgame ideas that are commonly overlooked.  Finally, the course concludes with remedies to three typical calculation mistakes. }
1. -- *

[Event "?"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "1. The Burger Technique"]
[Black "Introduction to The Burger Technique"]
[Result "*"]

{ Have you approached a position by immediately calculating the variations of the most tempting candidate moves, only to find out much later that you are barking up the wrong tree? Don’t worry, you are not alone. All too often, players fall victim to the lure of speed and are only too eager to calculate moves. As Mahatma Gandhi said “Speed is irrelevant if you are going in the wrong direction.”   So, how do we know which tree to bark up? Before deciding which moves to work on, we need to first understand the position’s static features, namely material count and tactical motifs. And in this chapter, I am going to introduce a systematic technique to do just that. I call it The Burger Technique.  The burger is used as a metaphor to denote the different steps involved. In its simplest form, a burger has three layers. Likewise, this technique comprises three steps. They are as follows:  1. Count the material and identify any material imbalance. For example, if you are down a Queen, you should be looking at mating attacks rather than gaining a piece or two. You can think of this as the topmost layer of the burger.  2. Identify the tactical motifs on the board. This covers a wide range of elements including, undefended/hanging pieces, existing/potential threats, and a vulnerable King position. When the correct motifs are identified, the best move will present itself. Because this is usually unfamiliar ground to most club players, we will devote the bulk of the chapter to this step.  Think of this as the core of the burger. Just as the flavour of the burger exudes from this layer, the success of your calculation largely hinges on how well this step is executed.  3. Finally, we arrive at the bottom layer of the burger. This step requires you to calculate candidate moves based on the motifs you have identified. If there is nothing compelling, consider the following types of moves in this order: Checks, captures, threats.  I cannot over-emphasise the need for these steps to be taken sequentially. By skipping steps 1 and 2, you will likely miss what the position is telling you and hence have difficulty in finding candidate moves systematically.  In this chapter, I will present a total of 30 puzzles divided into 8 thematic sets for you to practice The Burger Technique. The theme of each set can be found in the “informational” text page.  For your easy reference, the three steps are presented at the start of each puzzle. To guide you along, I may insert prompting questions and hints. You may toggle the ‘eye’ icon to hide or reveal them. The answers and explanations of each move will only be revealed after the puzzle is completed.  The Burger Technique is probably new to you and so it is perfectly normal to start slow. Hence, before we move on, I would like to give a word of advice. The emphasis should be on following through the three steps conscientiously rather than hastily. It may be tempting to skip the steps but I assure you that the results will be worth it if you persist. After all, this technique serves as the bedrock on which we will build to solve more complex positions in subsequent chapters. In time to come, you will develop a clearer thought process and calculate more accurately. Speed will take care of itself. Without further ado, let’s turn on the fire and start cooking. }
1. -- *

[Event "?"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "1. The Burger Technique"]
[Black "Identifying and Prioritising Unprotected Pieces"]
[Result "*"]

{ To find tactics that gain material effectively, you will first need to identify targets. There are many types of targets, but the first two common ones I’d like to introduce are unprotected pieces and hanging pieces. An unprotected piece has the same number of attackers and defenders, while a hanging piece has more attackers than defenders.  It is abundantly clear why hanging pieces can lead to material loss or gain, but what about unprotected pieces? Why are they considered to be targets if they appear to be sufficiently defended with one defender for every attacker? The reason is that they are ALMOST hanging. If anything untoward happens to a defender, or if a new attacker joins the party, the unprotected piece becomes a hanging piece, and is ripe for the taking.  We will occasionally find positions where it is difficult to divide our attention between multiple unprotected pieces to find the best tactic. In such situations, I recommend that you prioritise the targets to identify the unprotected piece that will most likely fall. You can do this by looking for the unprotected piece with the most attackers/defenders. The greater the number of attackers/defenders an unprotected piece has, the more likely you can harass one of the defenders to turn the unprotected piece into a hanging piece. Moreover, with more attackers, you have more ways to capture the piece. By calculating different move orders, you may find a line that leads to a favourable material trade. }
1. -- *

