[Black "Puzzle #1"]
[Result "*"]
[FEN "3r2k1/p1rbnpbp/1p2p1p1/1N6/1P1N4/4PB2/P4PPP/2RR2K1 b - - 1 1"]
[SetUp "1"]

{ 1.	Count the material and identify any material imbalance  2.	Identify tactical motifs     a.	  Unprotected/hanging pieces    b.	  Existing/potential threats  3.	Calculate candidate moves  
Here is our first puzzle, and we can use The Burger Technique to solve it.   Step 1 is counting the material. We can quickly confirm that material is equal, with a minor piece imbalance where Black has a dark-squared bishop against White's Knight.   Step 2 is to identify the tactical ideas in the position. Right now, we are only concerned with unprotected/hanging pieces. The hanging pieces will reveal the existing threats in the position, while the unprotected pieces indicate the potential threats. To recap, an unprotected piece has the same number of attackers and defenders, while a hanging piece has more attackers than defenders. Existing/potential threats are self-explanatory.    In this position, only Black's c7 Rook is hanging. The unprotected pieces are Black's d8 Rook, e7 Knight, and White's c1 Rook and b5 Knight. The question is, which of these unprotected pieces are most vulnerable? Since the d8 Rook and the e7 Knight have no attackers, they are less likely to be targets. On the other hand, the c1 Rook and the b5 Knight have one attacker and one defender, so they are more likely to be targets.   Now, let us examine the existing/potential threats. White's immediate threat is to capture on c7, and has a hidden potential threat with his d1 Rook opposite Black's unprotected d8 Rook. Black's potential threats are related to White's unprotected pieces established earlier.   Finally, in step 3, we can begin examining candidate moves. With all the ideas we have generated, find the best move in the position. }
1... Rxc1
{ This is a good candidate move to consider, because it takes care of Black's hanging piece and is quite a forcing move. More importantly, it does not give White any time to take care of his unprotected b5 Knight. }
2. Rxc1
{ With the tension between the Rooks resolved, we can now concentrate on the b5 Knight. This includes both its potential and current attackers and defenders. }
2... e5
{ Without any way to effectively add more attackers to b5, the next thing to consider is to remove its defenders.  If you were unable to find this move even with the help of the first two steps, don't worry. You can still lean on step 3. Working your way through the candidate moves in this order: checks, captures, and threats, you will realise that there are no possible checks. As for captures, you may briefly entertain the thought of   2...Bxd4  but quickly realise that it can be met with  3.Nxd4  Finally, by looking for threats, we can find ...e5 }
3. Nxa7 exd4 { And Black wins a piece for a pawn. } *

