{ 1.	Count the material and identify any material imbalance  2.	Identify tactical motifs     a.	  Unprotected/hanging pieces    b.	  Existing/potential threats  3.	Calculate candidate moves  Step 1: Material is equal.   Step 2: The f1 Rook seems to be protected because it is attacked by the high-valued c4 Queen and defended by the low-valued d1 Rook. However, by definition, the f1 Rook is an unprotected piece given that there are two attackers and two defenders. Finally, Black's King position is slightly weak.   Step 3: Based on these motifs, the winning move presents itself. }
1... Rf8 { Simply skewering the f3 Queen and the f1 Rook } 2. Qd3 Rxf1
{ 2...Qxd3  will give White time to play  3.Rxf8+  Rxf8  4.cxd3  and we do not win any material }
3. Rxf1 { The rest of the line is easy to work out } 3... Qxd3 4. cxd3 Bxd3+ 5.
Kc1 Bxf1
{ Hopefully, this puzzle has shown the importance of identifying all kinds of unprotected pieces, including those that are attacked by stronger pieces and defended by weaker ones. }
*

