{ 1. Count the material and identify any material imbalance  2. Identify tactical motifs    a. Unprotected/hanging pieces    b. Existing/potential threats    c. X-Ray attacks     d. Mating possibilities  3. Calculate candidate moves.  Step 1: Black is up one pawn.   Step 2: There are no hanging pieces, but White has three undefended spots - both of his bishops and the h2 pawn. Black's f7 pawn is also unprotected. There may possibly be some tension between the Rooks on the e-file with only one Bishop in the way. Black's dark squares are also very tender with White's h6 Bishop staring down at the King position.  Step 3: We can begin to examine candidate moves. When you are ready, make the best move for Black. }
1... Qh4 { Given the ideas generated from step 2, Qh4 presents itself. } 2.
Rxe5
{ g3 allows black to obtain his advantage very simply. Therefore, white's best try is to remove black's dark squared Bishop and hope that his Bishop pair is enough compensation.   2.g3  Qxh6   2...Qxc4??  3.Rxe5  Black has to deal with the threat of Qf6   2.Bd5  Qxh2+  3.Kf1  Qh1+  4.Ke2  Bf4+   2.Bb5  Qxh2+  3.Kf1  Qh1+  4.Ke2  Bf4+ }
2... dxe5
{ 2...Rxe5  3.Qxa8+   2...dxe5  3.Bd5  White's best try. Saving a hanging Bishop while attacking the Rook. Black has two threats, Qxh6 and Bh5, while white only has one, Bxa8. Therefore, Black can choose between a few moves to obtain a sizeable material advantage.  3...Qxh6  I chose this line because it does not require black to play Bh5 at any point. This means f7 will remain protected.   3...Rad8  4.Be3  Bh5  5.Qf5  Although this position is still winning for Black, it is not as simple because we cannot take on d1  5...Bxd1  6.Bxf7+  4.Bxa8  e4  Interfering the Queen's defence to the Bishop.  5.Bxe4  Bxe4  A slightly more accurate move than 5...Rxe4 to avoid Rd8+ followed by Qc3. }
