{ 1. Count the material and identify any material imbalance  2. Identify tactical motifs    a. Unprotected/hanging pieces    b. Existing/potential threats    c. X-Ray attacks     d. Mating possibilities  3. Calculate candidate moves.  Step 1: In this position, Black is up one pawn.   Step 2: All of White's pieces are overprotected. On the other hand, Black has an unprotected a8 Rook, d7 Queen, and d4 Bishop. Furthermore, with our Queen and Bishop covering h7, his a8 Rook is tied to the back rank.   Step 3: Based on what we have discussed in Step 2, the first move to consider should be quite clear. If not, you can work through the checks, captures and threats in this position. }
1. Qa4
{ Forking the two unprotected pieces! The Queen cannot be captured right now because of back rank mate. Since our Queen is almost hanging and Black's d7 Queen is indirectly defending d4, we need to ensure that Black has no tactical shot in this position before playing Qa4.   1...Rxa4  2.Rb8+  Qe8  3.Rxe8# }
1... Rd8
{ Attempting to set up a discovered attack once our Queen captures on d4 } 2.
Qxd4 { However, the Queen controls all of the d5 Knight's checking squares }
2... Nf4+ 3. Qxf4 { White will be up a piece for a pawn after 3...Qxd3 } *

