{ 1. Count the material and identify any material imbalance  2. Identify tactical motifs    a. Unprotected/hanging pieces    b. Existing/potential threats    c. X-Ray attacks     d. Mating possibilities  3. Calculate candidate moves.  Step 1: Black is up one pawn.   Step 2: Black's d8 Rook is hanging while White's d4 Rook is unprotected. Even though the e4 Knight has two defenders and one attacker, he is a target. This is because one of its defenders, the d4 Rook, can be traded off. White's threat is to capture the d8 Rook while Black's potential threats involve White's unprotected pieces and the moves Qf4+, Rc8, and Ba3+.   Step 3: Keeping all this in mind, find a few candidate moves and pick the best. }
1... Ba3+
{ Of all the possible checks, it is best to start with this one. The reason is that it defends the d8 Rook and activates all of Black's pieces }
2. Kb1
{ Since we have already identified that the e4 Knight and d4 Rook are soft targets, it is clear that the White King cannot step on the d-file. Here, we find ourselves potentially facing a roadblock. There are no more checks and we may not know how to proceed. This is when we should repeat the three-step thought process. We can easily breeze through the first two steps given that not much has changed from the initial position - the e4 Knight and d4 Rook are still targets. The two key changes are 1. Our d8 Rook is now defended. 2. White now has back rank issues. When we consider which of our major pieces can access the back rank, the solution becomes clear.   2.Kd1  Qxe4  3.Rxd8+  Rxd8+  White does not have time to take our Queen. }
2... Qxe4 { Our d8 Rook has an X-ray attack on the d1 square. } 3. Rxd8+
{ 3.Rxe4  Rd1 } 3... Rxd8
{ Similar to the variation analysed earlier, Qxe4 is still not possible because of Rd1# }
4. Qxe4 Rd1# *

