[White "1. The Burger Technique"]
[Black "Rejecting Moves "]
[Result "*"]

{ Having gone through the earlier puzzles, I hope you see the benefit of counting material imbalances and identifying tactical motifs before choosing candidate moves. Unsurprisingly, your opponent could also use the same three-step thought process on you. This is why before you make a move, you also need to think through these three steps from your opponent’s perspective. If you can find a satisfactory response from your opponent, you should entertain other candidate moves.  The next puzzle requires you to reject tempting candidate moves by going through the three steps on behalf of your opponent. }
1. -- *

[Event "?"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "1. The Burger Technique"]
[Black "Puzzle #13"]
[Result "*"]
[FEN "2r3k1/3R1p2/p4rpp/3Q4/8/P7/4qPPP/5RK1 b - - 1 1"]
[SetUp "1"]

{ 1. Count the material and identify any material imbalance  2. Identify tactical motifs    a. Unprotected/hanging pieces    b. Existing/potential threats    c. X-Ray attacks     d. Mating possibilities  3. Calculate candidate moves and reject poor moves by repeating the three-step thought process from the opponent’s perspective.  After working out the material count and the unprotected pieces, we would have some idea of what the tactic involves. }
1... Rc1
{ Not a capture or a check, which makes this move difficult to find. So what clues were there in this position to make us even consider this move? We had to first look through two very tempting candidate moves   1...Qxf2+  2.Rxf2  Rc1+  3.Qd1  Rxd1+  4.Rxd1  and the more complex   1...Rxf2  As mentioned in the previous page, the way to determine if this move is good is to try to solve the tactic from White's point of view. First, by looking at the motifs @@StartBracket@@f2 is hanging, the back rank is weak@@EndBracket@@, and then by studying the possible Checks, it is clear that the position favours White after Rxf2  2.Rd8+  Kg7  3.Qd4+  Notice the two undefended spots and that the back rank needs the f1 Rook, which makes it overloaded. Hence, the move Rc1 presents itself. }
2. Rxc1
{ 2.Rd1  Qe1+   2...Qxf2+  if you are looking for the same tactical motif }
2... Qxf2+ 3. Kh1 Qf1+ 4. Rxf1 Rxf1# *

[Event "?"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "1. The Burger Technique"]
[Black "When to Reject Moves"]
[Result "*"]

{ Amateur players miss countless tactics because they reject moves too hastily. A common piece of advice most coaches often dispense is to simply think deeper. However, it is impossible to work through every move exhaustively. For one, this is an overly demanding mental feat. It would also take too much time.   Let me introduce a practical guide on how much you should analyse a given variation. Before deciding to reject any move, consider at least one-and-a-half moves ahead and carry out an evaluation of the static elements. This refers to king safety, quantity of material, and quality of material.  If you think the resulting position is losing, confirm that it indeed cannot be salvaged with any desperate attempts. You can then safely reject the move.  If you think you are winning, do not be conceited. You should certainly look through the variation a second time to check that your opponent does not have any defensive resources. You can then play out the win.  However, if you think the resulting position is close to equal or unclear, you should continue to look for checks, captures and threats. For as long as these resources are available, continue to explore them until they are exhausted. At that point, conduct another evaluation of the static elements to determine if the initial move is good.  I will present a few more puzzles to help you familiarise with this technique. Go through the drill conscientiously. Remember, never rush to reject or play a move. }
1. -- *

[Event "?"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "1. The Burger Technique"]
[Black "Puzzle #14"]
[Result "*"]
[FEN "2r5/4k3/1P2b1R1/p2p4/P2p4/3P1B2/1K6/8 w - - 5 57"]
[SetUp "1"]

{ 1. Count the material and identify any material imbalance  2. Identify tactical motifs    a. Unprotected/hanging pieces    b. Existing/potential threats    c. X-Ray attacks     d. Mating possibilities  3. Calculate candidate moves and reject poor moves by repeating the three-step thought process from the opponent’s perspective. Be sure to calculate at least one and a half moves ahead before deciding to reject it.  Step 1: Material is equal with no imbalance.   Step 2: All pieces and pawns are unprotected except for the Black c8 Rook. However, since its only defender, the e6 Bishop, is under attack, the Rook remains a target. White's potential threats are Rxe6+ and Rg7+. On top of that, the b6 pawn is far advanced and dangerous.   Step 3: Find a few candidate moves based on the ideas we have discussed and pick the best. If no ideas come to mind, consider checks and captures first. }
57. Rxe6+
{ This should be the first move to consider, because it is the most forcing. Some players might reject this move immediately because it is losing an exchange in a very simplified position. However, this is premature because they have only looked half a move ahead. We should play out two more half moves before dismissing the move as a bad one. }
57... Kxe6 58. Bg4+ { Getting the material back with interest. } 58... Ke7 59.
Bxc8
{ This is a rather simple tactic. The takeaway is that rejecting your candidate moves without considering any possible continuations is a sure way to miss tactics. }
*

[Event "?"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "1. The Burger Technique"]
[Black "Puzzle #15"]
[Result "*"]
[FEN "3q1r1k/5bRp/5P2/7P/1P1ppN2/2Q1P3/5r2/2K4R w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]

{ 1. Count the material and identify any material imbalance  2. Identify tactical motifs    a. Unprotected/hanging pieces    b. Existing/potential threats    c. X-Ray attacks     d. Mating possibilities  3. Calculate candidate moves and reject poor moves by repeating the three-step thought process from the opponent’s perspective. Be sure to calculate at least one and a half moves ahead before deciding to reject it.  Step 1: White is up one pawn and has a Knight for Black's light-squared Bishop.   Step 2: White's Queen is hanging and the rest of his pieces are unprotected. On the other hand, Black only has his f2 Rook and f7 Bishop unprotected. White has chances for a good King hunt because almost his entire army is surrounding the enemy King.   Step 3: Find a few candidate moves and pick the best. Be sure to pay special attention to the checks, especially given that Black's King position is suspect. }
1. Ng6+
{ This move is one of three possible checks. It is important to follow the guideline of considering at least one-and-a-half moves ahead before rejecting the other moves   1.Rxh7+  Kxh7  Since we are down a significant amount of material and cannot even find any desperate moves, we can terminate our analysis here.   1.Rg8+  Rxg8  2.Ng6+  We have now calculated one-and-a-half moves into the line and have to decide whether to continue calculating or to reject this line. Since we were able to play a very forcing check and might have the h-file opened up, it is worth further analysis.  2...Bxg6  3.hxg6  Rxg6  We are losing quite badly. However, we should leave no stone unturned and explore the only check that we have in this position.  4.Rxh7+  Kxh7  With only a Queen left and no good checks, we can safely reject 1. Rg8 }
1... Bxg6
{ This move is virtually forced since 1...hxg6 leads to mate   1...hxg6  2.hxg6+  Rh2  3.Rxh2 }
2. hxg6
{ For similar reasons to what we discussed in the earlier lines, 2. Rxh7+ and 2. Rg8+ do not work.   2.Rxh7+  Bxh7   2.Rg8+  Rxg8 }
2... Rf1+ { Again, this move is forced because of the threat of Rhxh7# } 3.
Rxf1
{ 3.Kc2  Rxh1  Looking half a move deeper, we realise that there are no Queen moves that can avoid the Queen trade and stop 4...Qxf6, removing any chances of an attack from White }
3... dxc3
{ It is a pity that many weaker players stop their calculations here, concluding that White is down too much material. However, since there are still a few reasonable checks to be played out, this position is worth further analysis. }
4. Rxh7+ Kg8 5. Rg7+ Kh8 6. Rh1#
{ This is a relatively simple mating combination since all moves come with check. Yet, many players terminate their analyses prematurely. Hence, for as long as you are able to find reasonable checks, you should continue to explore the line. }
*

