[SetUp "1"]

{ 1. Count the material and identify any material imbalance  2. Identify tactical motifs    a. Unprotected/hanging pieces    b. Existing/potential threats    c. X-Ray attacks     d. Mating possibilities  3. Calculate candidate moves and reject poor moves by repeating the three-step thought process from the opponent’s perspective. Be sure to calculate at least one and a half moves ahead before deciding to reject it.  
Step 1: Material is equal, with both sides having opposite-coloured bishops.   Step 2: Both of Black's rooks and White's d4 bishop are unprotected. White's d1 Rook is also a potential target with Black's Rook at d8 placed opposite it. White's main threat is to create a discovered attack on the d8 Rook by moving his d4 bishop away with tempo. However, with all the Black pieces placed on light squares, this seems unlikely. Black's threats are Rxd4 and Bc4+. Additionally, there is an outside chance of a back rank mate, given that e2 is covered right now.   Step 3: With the help of what we have discussed in step 2, find a few candidate moves and pick the best. }
23... Bc4+
{ This is the first candidate move that we should consider because it is a check and one of our potential threats. It is also perfectly alright if you decided to look at 1...Rxf2 first, as long as you reject it only after looking through one-and-a half moves.   23...Rxf2+  24.Bxf2  Black is down a lot of material and there are no suitable desperate moves. Hence, 1...Rxf2 can be rejected }
24. Kg1
{ Many players might dismiss 1...Bc4+ as an innocuous check and stop their calculation right here. However, as discussed earlier, we should look at least another half move more. There are no more checks, but there are certainly still some captures and threats. Keeping in mind that d4 remains unprotected, we can find a very deadly threat here. }
24... Be2
{ Attacking the sole defender of d4, and the d1 Rook can no longer stay in touch with the d4 Bishop. Having calculated one-and- a-half moves ahead, we realise that Bc4+ was a purposeful check and we can correctly decide to play it out. The rest of the line is easy to work through. }
25. Rc1 Rxc1 26. Rxc1 Rxd4 { And we win a whole piece. } *

