[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "1. The Burger Technique"]
[Black "Puzzle #17"]
[Result "*"]
[FEN "r3rbk1/p5pp/2p2n2/4pN2/8/2N2Q2/PPq2PPP/3RR1K1 w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]

{ 1. Count the material and identify any material imbalance  2. Identify tactical motifs    a. Unprotected/hanging pieces    b. Existing/potential threats    c. X-Ray attacks    d. Mating possibilities    e. Trappable pieces  3. Calculate candidate moves and reject poor moves by repeating the three-step thought process from the opponent’s perspective. Be sure to calculate at least one and a half moves ahead before deciding to reject it.  Step 1: Material is equal, and White has a Knight for Black's Bishop.   Step 2: The only unprotected pieces are the c2 Queen and f5 Knight. Since there are no obvious potential threats, we might think this is a serene position with no tactics. However, we have just learnt to look for trappable pieces.   Step 3: What is the trappable piece and how do we take away its squares? }
1. Re2
{ The c2 Queen is extremely low on squares, with only c2 and b2 available to it. Hence, Re2 presents itself. }
1... Qxd1+ { 1...e4  can be simply met with any logical Queen move  2.Qh3 } 2.
Nxd1 { And White has a substantial material advantage } *

