[Black "Puzzle #18"]
[Result "*"]
[FEN "rnb1k2r/p1pp1pp1/1p2p2p/4P3/1b1P1q2/2NB1N2/PPP2PPP/R2QK2R w KQkq - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]

{ 1. Count the material and identify any material imbalance  2. Identify tactical motifs    a. Unprotected/hanging pieces    b. Existing/potential threats    c. X-Ray attacks    d. Mating possibilities    e. Trappable pieces  3. Calculate candidate moves and reject poor moves by repeating the three-step thought process from the opponent’s perspective. Be sure to calculate at least one and a half moves ahead before deciding to reject it.  Step 1: Material is balanced, and White, again, has a Knight for Black's dark-squared Bishop.   Step 2: White's only weakness is the unprotected c3 Knight. Black, on the other hand, has to deal with his unprotected a8 Rook and his trappable f4 Queen. To work out how the Queen can be trapped,  identify all the safe squares it can go to.   Step 3: After you identify all the safe squares for the Queen, find the best move in the position. }
1. g3
{ The Queen's only safe squares are f4 and g4. From g4, it can only go to h5, its last escape square. Hence, the idea is clearly to attack those three squares @@StartBracket@@f4, g4, h5@@EndBracket@@. Given that we prefer to calculate forcing moves before all else, we should try to control those squares with tempo. }
1... Qg4 { forced } 2. h3 Qh5 { forced } 3. g4 { and we win the Queen. } *

[Event "?"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "1. The Burger Technique"]
[Black "Puzzle #19"]
[Result "*"]
[FEN "rnb1k1nr/pp1p1ppp/4p3/2b5/3NP3/2N1B3/PqP2PPP/R2QKB1R w KQkq - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]

{ 1. Count the material and identify any material imbalance  2. Identify tactical motifs    a. Unprotected/hanging pieces    b. Existing/potential threats    c. X-Ray attacks    d. Mating possibilities    e. Trappable pieces  3. Calculate candidate moves and reject poor moves by repeating the three-step thought process from the opponent’s perspective. Be sure to calculate at least one and a half moves ahead before deciding to reject it.  Step 1: White is down a pawn.   Step 2: The unprotected pieces are White's a1 Rook, and Black's c5 Bishop and b2 Queen. Also, White's c3 Knight is hanging. All the potential threats are based around the unprotected pieces. Although it is not obvious, Black's b2 Queen counts as a trappable piece. For such a mobile piece, it strangely does not have many squares to run to. Its safe squares are b2, c3, a3, b4, and b6.   Step 3: Keeping the b2 Queen's escape squares in mind, find a move to eliminate as many of them as possible, preferably with tempo. }
1. Ndb5
{ In a single move, White has taken away three of the Queen's safe squares, leaving it with only b2 and b4. This means that Rb1 on the next move will trap the Queen. Furthermore, White is threatening Bxc5 and Nc7+. With so many threats, Black's only chance is a counterattack. It is not possible to parry all these threats through passive moves. }
1... Bb4
{ This directly avoids White's threat of Bxc5. On top of that, White cannot play Nc7+ or Rb1 immediately because of Bxc3+. However, this means that White simply has to defend c3 and Black will still have to deal with these dual threats. }
2. Bd2 { 2.Bd4  works as well } 2... Na6 { Stopping Nc7+ } 3. Rb1
{ And we win the Queen. I hope that this puzzle has shown you the importance of not only identifying the trappable piece, but also its escape squares. }
*

