[SetUp "1"]

{ 1. Count the material and identify any material imbalance  2. Identify tactical motifs    a. Unprotected/hanging pieces    b. Existing/potential threats    c. X-Ray attacks    d. Mating possibilities    e. Trappable pieces  3. Calculate candidate moves and reject poor moves by repeating the three-step thought process from the opponent’s perspective. Be sure to calculate at least one and a half moves ahead before deciding to reject it.  Step 1: Black is up two pawns.   Step 2: The main target is clearly the a2 Queen, who is both unprotected and trappable. Her escape squares are b2, a3, and a4.   Step 3: How do we control those squares? }
1. Qc3
{ It is important to recognise that the Black Queen cannot use the a4 escape square on pain of Bb5+. That is one less square to worry about. With 1. Qc3, we remove all the remaining escape squares - b2 and a3. We will have 2. Ra1 to trap the Queen next.   1.Bb5+??  It is not necessary to invest a move in Bb5 when the Queen hasn't declared her intention to use the a4 square yet.  1...Bd7  2.Qc3  Rc8 }
1... Bd7 2. Ra1 { We trap the Queen } 2... Qxa1+ 3. Qxa1
{ And we win the Queen } *

[Event "?"]
[Site "?"]
