[Round "?"]
[White "1. The Burger Technique"]
[Black "Puzzle #21"]
[Result "*"]
[FEN "rnb1kb1r/p5p1/2p1p1n1/1p1pPpNp/3q1B2/2NB4/PPPQ1PPP/1R3RK1 w kq - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]

{ 1. Count the material and identify any material imbalance  2. Identify tactical motifs    a. Unprotected/hanging pieces    b. Existing/potential threats    c. X-Ray attacks    d. Mating possibilities    e. Trappable pieces  Step 1: Black is up one pawn.   Step 2: White's f4 Bishop is hanging while Black's d4 Queen, g6 Knight, c8 Bishop, and a8 Rook are unprotected. Additionally, the Black Queen is somewhat trappable. What are her safe squares and escape routes?   Step 3: Find a few candidate moves and pick the best. }
1. Bxf5
{ A quick look at the Black Queen's escape squares reveal that she is, in fact, not trappable. There is no way for us to deal with our hanging Bishop and take away all the escape squares at once - b4, c5, b6, and perhaps even e5.   So our focus is not to trap the Queen. Rather, our goal is to take advantage of the unprotected pieces. With this mindset shift, we can see that two of Black's unprotected pieces @@StartBracket@@d4 Queen and g6 Knight@@EndBracket@@, are under an X-ray attack. Hence, those are the main targets and we are able to easily find this killer tactic.     1.Nxb5??  Qxf4  2.Nc7+  Kd7  Avoiding the Nf7 fork  3.Qxf4  Nxf4  4.Nxa8  Black will eventually win our trapped Knight and have two pieces for the Rook }
1... Qxd2
{ 1...Qxf4  2.Bxg6+  Kd7  3.Qxf4   1...Qb6  2.Bxg6+   1...Ne7  2.Qxd4 } 2.
Bxg6+ { This zwischenzug wins us a Knight } 2... Kd7 3. Bxd2 *

