{ 1. Count the material and identify any material imbalance  2. Identify tactical motifs    a. Unprotected/hanging pieces    b. Existing/potential threats    c. X-Ray attacks    d. Mating possibilities    e. Trappable pieces    f. Pinned, pinnable, forkable, and skewerable pieces  3. Calculate candidate moves and reject poor moves by repeating the three-step thought process from the opponent’s perspective. Be sure to calculate at least one and a half moves ahead before deciding to reject it.  Step 1: White is down a Rook and a pawn.   Step 2: Only the g7 Bishop is unprotected.  As for the rest of the threats and tactical motifs, I will leave them as an exercise for you. Be sure to consider the new motifs we have learnt.   Step 3: Find the best move. }
1. Qe7#
{ This is a tactic that even decent players can sometimes miss. So it is important to develop the habit of identifying all pinned, and hence “deactivated” pieces. }
