[White "1. The Burger Technique"]
[Black "Puzzle #24"]
[Result "*"]
[FEN "r5k1/pp2qpbp/1np1r1p1/8/2P1B2N/1P4Pb/P1QB1P1P/1R2R1K1 b - - 5 1"]
[SetUp "1"]

{ 1. Count the material and identify any material imbalance  2. Identify tactical motifs    a. Unprotected/hanging pieces    b. Existing/potential threats    c. X-Ray attacks    d. Mating possibilities    e. Trappable pieces    f. Pinned, pinnable, forkable, and skewerable pieces  3. Calculate candidate moves and reject poor moves by repeating the three-step thought process from the opponent’s perspective. Be sure to calculate at least one and a half moves ahead before deciding to reject it.  
Step 1: Material is equal.   Step 2: The unprotected pieces are Black's h3 Bishop, and White's h4 Knight and e4 Bishop. The e7 Queen is a target since it is placed opposite the e1 Rook. Conversely, even though the e1 Rook is overprotected with two defenders and no attackers, it remains a target. This is because Black has two X-ray attackers, the e6 Rook and e7 Queen. Thanks to the h3 Bishop, White also has a weak back rank. Therefore, the e4 Bishop is a pinnable piece.   Step 3: Bearing all this in mind, the first move to consider should be quite clear. }
1... Re8
{ Pinning the Bishop and attacking it one more time. There is no escape from the dual threats of ...Rxe4 and ...f5 }
2. f3 f5
{ As you can see, by identifying the e4 Bishop as a pinnable piece, we can easily find the best move in the position. }
*

