[Black "Chapter Conclusion"]
[Result "*"]

{ Congratulations! You have completed the first chapter of Calculation: A Complete Guide for Tournament Players.   As you may have noticed, The Burger Technique is most effective in situations that demand accuracy over speed, for example, in slow games or puzzles without a time limit. For the time being, the aim is to use this technique as a form of deliberate practice in identifying the tactical clues and motifs in positions. Eventually, you will develop the ability to do so subconsciously in much faster time controls. Moreover, your sharpened instincts will enable you to smell blood and wisely invest your time at critical junctures to look for the kill.  In the next few chapters, we will be building on what we have learnt to improve our efficiency and speed. Now that you have memorised the three steps of the technique, it is no longer necessary for me to repeat it at the start of all commentaries for the rest of the course. However, you should continue to mentally go through the drill in tandem with new techniques I will introduce along the way. }
1. -- *

[Event "?"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "2. Reciprocal Thinking"]
[Black "Introduction to Reciprocal Thinking"]
[Result "*"]

{ I am sure many of you have heard that candidate moves should strictly be calculated in this order: Checks, captures, threats. This is because tactics often arise from these forcing moves. However, there are a few downsides to this method. For one, if the best move happened to be a “quiet” threat, much effort would be wasted calculating irrelevant checks and captures. Even worse, if the best move is defensive, then you are barking up the wrong tree if you consider only checks, captures, and threats.   This is why in the previous chapter I recommended that you adopt The Burger Technique:  (i)  counting the material,  (ii)  analysing tactical motifs, and  (iii)  identifying compelling candidate moves regardless of how forcing they are. For the most part, this approach works well. However, even after completing the first two steps, there could be situations where there are a myriad of candidate moves. The challenge is to find the best candidate move in an efficient way.  In this chapter, I will introduce a technique called reciprocal thinking to complement The Burger Technique. Reciprocal thinking can be implemented after calculating through your first candidate move. If that move fails, do not discard it and randomly hop to another. Instead, diagnose the cause of failure and only consider subsequent candidate moves that rectify the problem. If the new candidate move fixes the current problem but creates another, then look for yet other candidate moves that solve both these problems. Repeat this process until you find a move that gives no problems.   As one of the most powerful techniques, reciprocal thinking not only uncovers subtle moves, but also keeps us from examining random moves. To put that into practice, we will be looking through 25 puzzles. For the first three, I will guide you with a few hints on how reciprocal thinking can be integrated into The Burger Technique. Subsequently, you will solve the puzzles unaided and the ideal thought process will be revealed after each one is completed. }
1. -- *

[Event "?"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "2. Reciprocal Thinking"]
[Black "Puzzle #1"]
[Result "*"]
[FEN "3r2k1/pp1q3p/1b2b1p1/6Q1/4P3/1P6/PB6/K1N4R w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]

{ Step 1: Black is up one pawn, and has a light-squared Bishop for our Knight.   Step 2: The unprotected spots are Black's g6 pawn and our g5 Queen and h1 Rook. Additionally, a1-h8 is clearly a critical diagonal since we have an unopposed b2 Bishop sniping at Black's vulnerable King on g8.   Step 3: Identify a suitable candidate move. If it works, go for it! Otherwise, if Black has a defense, find another candidate move that combats that defense. }
1. Ne2
{ A simple prophylactic move against Bd4. Black has no way of answering the dual threats of Qe5 and Qf6   1.Qe5  There is a good chance you would have considered either 1. Qe5 or Qf6 first. Those are strong moves, and they threaten Qh8+ followed by Rxh7#. However, Black can easily diffuse our threat – by opposing our Bishop with his own  1...Bd4  This forces too many trades and the resulting endgame is clearly worse for White  2.Bxd4  Qxd4+  3.Qxd4  Rxd4-+   1.Qf6  Bd4  Black adopts the same defense }
1... Qd6
{ Defending e5, but leaving f6 unguarded   1...Rf8  Defending f6, but leaving e5 unguarded  2.Qe5 }
2. Qf6
{ Working through checks, captures, and threats sequentially does not help you find Ne2. However, with reciprocal thinking, this tough move becomes obvious. }
*

