[FEN "R5Q1/3bkr2/1p4pp/1P1Pp3/1pP1P3/3Bb2P/5qP1/7K w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]

{ Step 1: We are down a dark-squared Bishop for a pawn.   Step 2: Black has the direct Qg1# and is ready to play Bf4 should the King ever try to escape through h2. Clearly, our only chance is to go for a mating attack against Black's King, or at least give a perpetual check. Needless to say, all our moves must come with check to stave off Qg1#.   Step 3: Calculate forcing lines where all the moves come with check. Again, if the line leads to mate, go for it. However, if you find the King escaping death, combat his defensive resources in the subsequent candidate moves that you consider. }
1. Qd8+
{ This move is virtually forced. There are no other reasonable checks. } 1...
Kd6 2. c5+
{ This is the only tough move to spot. After noticing the King's escape route, we learn that this is the only way to seal it. Everything is easy to work out from here.  The first move you would have likely entertained is   2.Qb8+  Kc5  3.Qc7+  The only reasonable move  3...Kd4  But the King escapes through c5 and d4.  4.Qxb6+  Kxd3  We are out of checks. Black, on the other hand, threatens Qg1# and Bxb6. This is a lost position for us. }
2... Kxc5
{ 2...Bxc5  blocks the King's escape route  3.Qb8+  Ke7  4.Qxe5+   2...bxc5  And we have the immediate  3.Ra6  Or if you'd like this line to be consistent with the other variations   3.Qb8+  Ke7  4.Qxe5+  Be6  5.Qxe6 }
3. Qc7+ Kd4 4. Qc4# *

[Event "?"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "2. Reciprocal Thinking"]
[Black "Puzzle #3"]
[Result "*"]
[FEN "rn3r2/1p3pk1/2p1pN1p/p3Qbq1/3P4/8/PPP3BP/R4RK1 w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]

{ Step 1: We are down a pawn.   Step 2: The plan is clearly to either give checkmate or win material with the discovered attack set up against Black's g7 King.   Step 3: Consider candidate moves. One way to try and win the enemy Queen is Ne4+. How do you think Black would try to defend against that? }
1. Rxf5
{ Without first considering 1. Ne4+, the logic behind Rxf5 will sound absolutely ludicrous: The purpose of Rxf5 is to remove the key defender of e6. Let's explore 1. Ne4+ to find out why.   1.Ne4+  f6  Black must create a counterattack on our Queen since his own Queen cannot be saved.  2.Qc7+  We are not looking for a trade, so our Queen must run with check  2...Rf7  Again, Black must block the check with a counterattack on our Queen  3.Nxg5   3.Qxf7+  We can also try to win the exchange. But since we were down a pawn to begin with, this move isn't enough for a clear victory  3...Kxf7  4.Nxg5+  hxg5⩲  Rxc7  4.Rxf5  The idea is 4...exf5 5. Ne6+ where we will be up a piece for a pawn at the end. But why should Black trade his c7 Rook for our f5 Rook here when he can simply win the Knight with  4...hxg5  Therefore, Rxf5 must be played much earlier, before all this mess starts }
1... exf5
{ 1...Qxf5  loses control over g7  2.Ne8+  Kg6  3.Qg7+  Kh5  4.Nf6+  White is easily better after }
2. Ne4+ f6 3. Qc7+ Rf7 4. Nxg5 Rxc7
{ The exact same line, but Black has already committed to meeting Rxf5 with exf5 }
5. Ne6+
{ Now that you have an idea of how to employ reciprocal thinking, the explanations of the rest of the puzzles will only be revealed after they are completed. }
*

