[Event "?"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "2. Reciprocal Thinking"]
[Black "Puzzle #8"]
[Result "*"]
[FEN "2Q2R2/3p2p1/4ppqk/7p/3P1KbP/3BP1P1/8/8 b - - 1 1"]
[SetUp "1"]

1... Qe4+
{ The crux of the problem is the White King's escape square, e4. There are a number of lines that can bring you to that conclusion so long as reciprocal thinking is used.   1...Qf5+  2.Bxf5  e5+  To take the e5 square away. Again, this move can be discovered through reciprocal thinking in the line where we play the natural 2...g5   2...g5+  is too early because e5 will be another escape square for White  3.hxg5+  fxg5+  4.Ke5  3.dxe5  g5+  4.hxg5+  fxg5+  5.Ke4  And the King is saved thanks to this one square, e4  We can also arrive at the same idea if our first candidate move was 1...e5   1...e5+  2.dxe5  Qg5+  A brief glance at this line will show that White has a draw at worst, and further calculation reveals that he can escape the perpetual check   2...fxe5+  3.Kxe5  Qe6+  4.Kd4  Qd6+  5.Kc4  Be6+  6.Kc3  Qa3+  7.Kd2  Qb4+  8.Ke2  3.hxg5+  fxg5+  4.Ke4  We reach a similar position to the one after 1...Qf5+, where the e4 square shows to be the problem again }
2. Kxe4
{ Allows d5+ to come with tempo whilst covering the e4 square   2.Bxe4  blocks e4  2...e5+  3.dxe5  g5+  4.hxg5+  fxg5 }
2... d5+ 3. Kf4 e5+ { taking away e5 } 4. dxe5 g5+ 5. hxg5+ fxg5#
{ Yet another puzzle that shows the power of reciprocal thinking. Regardless of which natural first move we start on, we can still find our way to the best line. Moreover, reciprocal thinking was used twice here to find the checkmate, first exposing the e5 escape square, and thereafter exposing the e4 escape square. }
