[Event "?"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "2. Reciprocal Thinking"]
[Black "Puzzle #13"]
[Result "*"]
[FEN "8/3k1p2/P1p4P/1p6/8/N1b5/5K2/8 w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]

{ If the Black King ever catches the a-pawn on its own, then the c3 Bishop is free to watch over the h-pawn undistracted and the position will be unwinnable for us. }
1. Nxb5
{ The main idea is to divert the c6 pawn from the defense of d5. This move can only be found through logical reasoning and reciprocal thinking. Let's take a look at the intuitive 1. a7   1.a7  Bd4+  Forced. Otherwise we Queen.  2.Kf3  Bxa7  3.Ke4  Stopping the Bishop from returning to the a1-h8 diagonal  3...f5+  4.Ke5  Continuing to stop Bd4  4...Bc5  5.h7  Bxa3  6.h8=Q  Bb2+  But as it turns out, Black finds a way to bring his Bishop back in time.   From this, we learn a few ideas. First, our King must not step on the a1-h8 diagonal because it allows a skewer.   Second, the two squares we need to control are d4 and e5. From a7, the only way the Bishop can reach the a1-h8 diagonal in two moves or less is a7-d4 and a7-b8-e5.   Finally, the only light-square from which our King can defend both d4 and e5, namely e4, is unreliable because f5+ will displace the King.   What other light square could control d4 and e5? The answer is d5. That is why we should play 1. Nxb5 to win the d5 square for our King }
1... cxb5 2. a7 Bd4+ 3. Kf3 Bxa7 4. Ke4 f5+ 5. Kd5
{ Now we have this move. and the h-pawn is unstoppable } *

