{ This position is taken from the previous puzzle. With reciprocal thinking, we realised that all attempts but one resulted in the King running to safety via b8. From there, we looked for candidate moves to take care of that hole and we arrived at 1. Rb7. }
1. Rb7
{ While the technique works, there is a way to make it even more efficient: By first identifying the King’s escape squares. This way, we would not even need to consider a random candidate move at the start. Of course, this technique of identifying the King’s escape square should only be applied when the motif is a vulnerable enemy King.  In the next few puzzles, we will be looking at King hunts. Be sure to identify the King’s escape squares first so that we will arrive at the solution much faster. }
