1. Qxg6+
{ With our King so exposed and Black threatening to play e1=Q, our only hope is a King hunt and all our moves should come with check. Getting rid of the defender of g7 makes the most sense.   1.Rxg7+  Rxg7  2.Rxg7+  Kxg7  leads to nothing }
1... Kxg6 2. Rxg7+ { The only good check } 2... Kf6
{ Here is where a lot of players go wrong with either Rgf7+ or Rbf7+. Both moves are hardly sensible because the King easily slides to e6 and then d6. Since Black is threatening both e1=Q+ and Qg3+, all our moves must come with check. This means that we have to cover both f6 @@StartBracket@@for the check@@EndBracket@@ and e6 @@StartBracket@@to cut the escape route@@EndBracket@@. Given that Rb6 is not possible, the only way to control those two squares is Rg6. And since the King is currently keeping an eye on g6, we need to shoo him away. }
3. g5+ hxg5 4. fxg5+ Ke6 5. Rg6# *

