[Event "?"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "2. Reciprocal Thinking"]
[Black "The Superposition Technique"]
[Result "*"]
[FEN "8/8/p5p1/5p2/PP6/2pK3P/6kP/8 w - - 1 1"]
[SetUp "1"]

{ To recap, reciprocal thinking requires you to calculate one line, check for its flaw, then find a second candidate move that corrects that flaw. However, there are peculiar instances where the resulting positions are hard to compare. Or there may be too many variations to consider within a single candidate move, let alone two or three.    This is why I have created the Superposition Technique which allows us to easily compare two or more candidate moves. The idea is simple: Whenever you have multiple similar candidate moves to consider, calculate all the lines simultaneously, then compare the end positions. Finally, pick the line with the best resulting position. This simple example will illustrate the thought process. }
1. b5 axb5
{ Here we have two moves to consider, 2. a5 and 2. axb5. Let's calculate both lines simultaneously to see which is better. 2. a5/axb5 f4 3. a6/b6 f3 4. a7/b7 f2 5. a8=Q+/b8=Q. Now it is clear that a5 is better since it comes with check and does not give Black time to play f1=Q+. Of course, we could have solved this through other methods, but the purpose is just to illustrate the concept of the Superposition Technique }
2. a5 { 2.axb5  f4  3.b6  f3  4.b7  f2  5.b8=Q  f1=Q+ } 2... f4 3. a6 f3 4. a7
f2
{ Be patient when learning this technique. Although not easy to grasp initially, I can assure you that with conscientious practice, you will develop far greater mental clarity than if you were to rely only on conventional calculation techniques. }
*

[Event "?"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "2. Reciprocal Thinking"]
[Black "Puzzle #21"]
[Result "*"]
[FEN "1k3r2/6RP/1p5n/7P/P1P5/1P6/2P2p2/5K2 b - - 1 1"]
[SetUp "1"]

1... Nf5
{ White was threatening to play Rg6 to chase the Knight from the defense of g8, and thereafter, play Rg8. The text move harasses the enemy Rook and prepares to displace the enemy monarch from c8, where it blocks our pawn. }
2. Rf7
{ If you considered this move before playing 1...Nf5, great job! This is a key variation to consider because it is rather tricky. We cannot take the Rook because of h8=Q+ followed by Kxf2. The only way is to give a Knight check, but which is better? Ng3+ or Ne3+? Is there a difference? Let's calculate them simultaneously with the Superposition Technique. 2...Ng3+/Ne3+ 3. Kxf2 @@StartBracket@@3. Kg2/Ke2 f1=Q+ 4. Rxf1 Nxf1@@EndBracket@@ 3...Rxf7+ 4.Kxg3/Kxe3 Rxh7 5. Kg4/Kf4. Comparing the two resulting positions, it is now clear that 2...Ne3+ is the better check because we can take on h5 at the end. In contrast, 2...Ng3 allows the King to defend h5 at the end, which will be a nuisance. }
2... Ne3+ { 2...Ng3+  3.Kxf2  Rxf7+  4.Kxg3  Rxh7  5.Kg4 } 3. Kxf2 Rxf7+ 4.
Kxe3 Rxh7 5. Kf4 Rxh5 *

[Event "?"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "2. Reciprocal Thinking"]
[Black "Puzzle #22"]
[Result "*"]
[FEN "2kr4/ppp5/5p2/2b5/2N1K3/8/PPP2r2/R3R2Q b - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]

Rd4+
{ A brief look at 1...f5 will show that the King easily escapes   1...f5+  2.Ke5  Bd4+  3.Ke6 }
2. Ke3
{ Here, our d4 Rook can unleash a myriad of discovered attack via two axes. This calls for the Superposition Technique. Let's start with the horizontal one. If 2...Rf4+/Rg4+, then 3. Kd3 and White is prepared to shield any horizontal checks with 4. Re3 or 4. Ne3. Even winning the Queen for the Rook with 2...Rh4+ is not enough because we are already down almost a whole Queen. If 2...Rxc4+, then 3. Kd3 Rd4+ 4. Kc3 and we are out of checks.   Let's now consider Rook checks along the vertical. For obvious reasons, I will not consider 2...Rd3+. 2...Rd1+/Rd2+/Rd5+/Rd6+/Rd7+/Rd8+ 3. Ke4 With White threatening Qh8+, our next move must come with check. In the line 2...Rd5+, the only check that saves our Rook is 3...Rd4+, which repeats the position. Keeping in mind that we have at least a draw, we will omit 2...Rd5+ in our Superposition calculation.   Resetting our calculation from move 2, after 2...Rd1+/Rd2+/Rd6+/Rd7+/Rd8+ 3. Ke4, the only good check is 3...f5+. This forces 4. Ke5 where the only good check is 4...Bd4+. Here, if the Rook were on d1 or d2, the King can escape through e6 or even d5. If the Rook were on d7 or d8, the King can escape through e6. But if our Rook were on d6, it would be checkmate. Therefore, 2...Rd6 is the answer. }
2... Rd6+
{ 2...Rh4+  3.Kd3  Rxh1  4.Rxh1+-   2...Rg4+  3.Kd3  Rg3+  4.Ne3   2...Rxc4+  3.Kd3  Rd4+  4.Kc3   2...Rd5+  3.Ke4  f5+  4.Kxd5   2...Rd1+  3.Ke4  f5+  4.Ke5  Bd4+  5.Ke6   2...Rd8+  3.Ke4  f5+  4.Ke5  Bd4+  5.Ke6 }
3. Ke4 f5+ 4. Ke5 Bd4# *

[Event "?"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "2. Reciprocal Thinking"]
[Black "Puzzle #23"]
[Result "*"]
[FEN "7B/7P/7k/p1Rp1K2/8/8/q1P1p3/8 w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]

{ Black is about to create a second Queen. Therefore, our only hope is the h-pawn, and we need to make use of it fast. }
1. Be5
{ Non-Bishop moves are far too slow. Rc6+ can be met with ...Kxh7 while other moves can be met with e1=Q. The question is, which square should we move our Bishop to? Bg7, Bb2, and Ba1 hang the Bishop outright while Bc3 can be met with ...Qxc2+. Hence, we are going to use the Superposition Technique to consider 1. Bf6/Be5/Bd4 all at once. Black is forced to play 1...Kxh7 and we have the powerful 2. Rc8!. Black has no way of stopping Rh8# other than 2...Qxc2+ 3. Rxc2 e1=Q 4. Rh1+ Kh7. In this resulting position, we need to cover the f7 escape square if we want to win the game. Our Rook will be busy guarding the 8th rank when it lands on h8 while our dark-squared Bishop cannot cover light squares. Furthermore, it must remain on the a1-h8 diagonal to protect the h8 square for the Rook. Therefore, it is up to our King to move forward and control f7. When we consider which variation, Bd4, e5, or f6, allows us to advance our King to control f7 safely, we realise that the Bishop is best placed on e5. This is because it allows us to safely play 5. Ke6 to block the f7 square, and we will have Rh8# next. }
1... Kxh7 2. Rc8 Qxc2+ 3. Rxc2 e1=Q 4. Rh2+ Kg8 5. Ke6 *

[Event "?"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "2. Reciprocal Thinking"]
[Black "Puzzle #24"]
[Result "*"]
[FEN "5K2/7k/8/6P1/8/pp1N3N/8/8 w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]

{ The Superposition Technique can be applied to opponent moves as well.   Here, a brief calculation reveals that Black's Queenside pawns are unstoppable. Hence, we must create a counterattack on the King instead. You may notice that  our effort to attack the King may be thwarted either by a1=Q or b1=Q, both of which  offer Black unique defensive value. It is up to you if you'd like to calculate them separately or simultaneously. }
1. Ndf4
{ This move controls g6 and weaves a mating net around the King. The f4 Knight controls g6 and h5 while the h3 Knight controls g5. 1. Kf7 allows Black to Queen his pawn while his King is free. Now comes the tricky part - We know that we are going to play g6-g7-g8, but which pawn will Black push? Let's use the Superposition Technique to consider both possibilities.   1...a2/b2 2. g6+Kh6 3. g7 a1=Q/b1=Q. Queening our own pawn will result in an unstoppable barrage of checks, so let's consider “Knighting” it with check instead. 4. g8=N+ Kh7 5. Ng5+ Kh8. If his Queen is on a1, then 6. Ng6#. If his Queen is on b1, then 6. Nf7+ Kh7 7. Nf6#.   1.Kf7  b2  2.g6+  Kh6  3.Nxb2  axb2  4.g7  b1=Q  5.g8=Q }
1... b2 { 1...a2  2.g6+  Kh6  3.g7  a1=Q  4.g8=N+  Kh7  5.Ng5+  Kh8  6.Ng6 } 2.
g6+ Kh6 3. g7 b1=Q 4. g8=N+ Kh7 5. Ng5+ Kh8 6. Nf7+ Kh7 7. Nf6# *

[Event "?"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "2. Reciprocal Thinking"]
[Black "Puzzle #25"]
[Result "*"]
[FEN "8/6pp/8/6PP/7P/8/p2K4/k7 w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]

{ There are positions where the Superposition Technique is ill-suited. The following puzzle is one such example.   The motifs and parameters of this puzzle are clear: We are deciding between Kc1 and Kc2 to put our opponent in semi-stalemate. The trick is to make sure that we leave Black with some legal pawn moves on the Kingside while ensuring that he does not promote in time or with a check on g1 or h1. With two similar candidate moves, is the Superposition Technique best applied here? }
1. Kc1
{ Using the Superposition Technique, it may get confusing as to which line originated from which move, since the King will be oscillating between c1 and c2 so much. Instead, we should focus on one line. If it is good, we should certainly go for it. Otherwise, we can use reciprocal thinking to determine if the other move is better. Without further ado, let's start calculating! I have chosen 1. Kc2 to start us off.   1.Kc2  g6   1...h6  Taking on h6 or playing 2. g6 clearly leads to stalemate. We must wait with our King.  2.Kc1  g6   2...hxg5  3.hxg5  g6  4.Kc2  gxh5  5.g6  h4  6.g7  h3  7.g8=Q  h2  8.Qg7  It's worthwhile noting that White wins in this line even if his King were on c1 now instead of c2.  3.Kc2   3.gxh6  gxh5   3.hxg6  h5  gxh5   3...hxg5  4.h6  gxh4  5.h7  h3  6.h8=Q  Again, our King could be on c1 here and we would still win.  4.Kc1  Continuing to avoid stalemate  4...hxg5  5.hxg5  h4  6.g6  h3  7.g7  h2  8.g8=Q  h1=Q+  Draw. Had we played 1. Kc1, our King would be on c2 now and we will have 9. Qg7#  2.Kc1  gxh5   2...h6  Transposes to the drawing line  3.Kc2  h6  4.Kc1  hxg5  5.hxg5  h4  6.g6  h3  7.g7  h2  8.g8=Q  h1=Q+  Draw. This would, again, have been a win if our King were on c2.     To conclude our calculation for 1. Kc2, we only have a draw. Those draws would have been wins if our King were on c2, avoiding a check when Black Queens. In the lines where we do win after 1. Kc2, we had enough extra tempi and would still win even if our King were on the other square. Hence, 1. Kc1 wins the game }
1... g6
{ 1...h6  2.Kc2  hxg5  3.hxg5  g6  4.Kc1  gxh5  5.g6  h4  6.g7  h3  7.g8=Q  h2  8.Qg7  Just in the nick of time }
2. Kc2 gxh5 { 2...h6  3.Kc1  hxg5   3...gxh5  4.Kc2  4.h6 } 3. Kc1 h6 4. Kc2
hxg5 5. hxg5 h4 6. g6 h3 7. g7 h2 8. g8=Q h1=Q 9. Qg7# *

[Event "?"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "3. Pattern Internalisation Essentials"]
[Black "Introduction to Pattern Internalisation Essentials"]
[Result "*"]

{ One of the key advantages a human has over chess engines is the ability to recognise patterns. After sufficient exposure to the game, you probably would have noticed a certain type of checkmate or an opening trap occurring rather frequently. Some examples include a back rank checkmate or a trick on the f7/f2 square. These positions and tactical combinations may have then left a deep impression on you. As a result, whenever a similar position presents itself again, you are able to execute the correct series of moves instinctively. This is how pattern recognition works.  The secret rapid improvement lies in the ability to recognise a given pattern after only few exposures. Hence, to expedite your chess growth, I have developed a method which I will call Pattern Internalisation Essentials  (PIE)  . PIE is intended to help you effectively internalise new tactical patterns for which you have just found the correct sequence of moves. It comprises the following four steps:  1. Notice how the relevant chess pieces interact. Take note of the responsibilities of every piece in making the tactic possible.  The more purposefully we pay attention to the dynamics between the pieces, the deeper we will appreciate the pattern. This enhances our ability to carry out the subsequent steps.  2. Determine the types of strategical weaknesses in your opponent’s position that pave the way for the tactic. This includes poor piece activity, weak squares, specific unprotected pieces, and many more.  By training ourselves to identify the preconditions for a tactical combination, we are adding to our repertoire of tactical motifs. Ultimately, this will sharpen our tactical vision. For example, whenever we see our opponent having three pawns on their original squares in front of his King, we would instinctively find ways to manoeuvre a major piece towards the back rank.  3. Consider changing the position slightly in a realistic manner. You may change, add, or remove certain critical pieces. Check if the tactic still works, or if a different tactic becomes available.  At first glance, this step may appear redundant. However, if you go through it diligently, you are poised to solve a wider range of similar positions from a single pattern. Take for example the back rank checkmate whose basic motif is three pawns in front of the King. Now, suppose one of the pawns had been shifted out of the second rank  (e.g. h2-h3)  , creating a flight square, h2, for the King. As long as this flight square is covered by one of our pieces  (e.g. Bb8 or Ng4)  , back rank checkmate remains a possibility. From this imaginative exercise arising from just one pattern  (three pawns in front of King)  , we are able to solve multiple positions may well present themselves from time to time in our games.   In fact, following through this step yields a bigger benefit. You will develop a deeper appreciation of what you found from the earlier two steps. Let me explain. Suppose you have changed the position by giving the opponent an extra dark-squared Bishop. And then you realise that the particular tactic no longer works. This highlights the value of your pieces controlling dark squares  (which you might have found in step 1)  , and confirms that weak dark squares a key strategical weakness in the opponent’s camp  (which you might have found in step 2)  .  4. Mentally play out the tactical combination a few times. Be sure to include critical variations when doing so.  Making mental moves repeatedly helps sear the combination in your mind and acquaints you with the key moves.  Unlike the methods presented in previous chapters, PIE can be approached a lot more flexibly. It is up to you how thoroughly and for how long you would like to execute each step. Using PIE on a single pattern can take anywhere between 20 seconds and 10 minutes. The whole idea is for you to have a deeper appreciation of the pattern.  To progressively familiarise you with PIE, this chapter is divided into four sections. In the first, I will show you a set of 10 common checkmating patterns. Working with familiar patterns, it will be easier for you to understand how PIE can be used. I will share my own thought process in detail as I work through each step. Bear in mind that there are dozens of ways to carry out each step for any given pattern. So don’t be afraid to come up with your own ideas.  In the second section, you will be given 5 relevant puzzles to put what you have learnt to practice. This is the “Aha!” moment for many students because they realise that PIE has enabled them to sense obscure mating possibilities in otherwise difficult positions. As a result, they are able to steer the game to desirable positions with ease.  In the third section, we will then return to using PIE on another set of 10 patterns. But this time, they are significantly more complex and are taken from real games. This will give you a better idea of how to use PIE in practical situations. And in the final section, we will solve another 5 puzzles related to these 10 complex patterns. }
1. -- *

