[Black "Clues, Goals, and Exceptions"]
[Result "*"]
[FEN "rr4k1/q4ppp/8/1b6/3Q2N1/8/3R1PPP/1B1R2K1 w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]

{ When you apply PIE to any pattern, three key elements will become salient to you: The clues, the goals, and the exceptions.  In the case of this back rank mate, the clue is the three pawns directly in front of the Black King. The goal is then to move one of your major pieces to the back rank safely. Since we have three major pieces attacking d8 while Black only has two, Qd8+ would initially appear to be a good sacrifice leading to mate. }
1. Qxa7
{ 1.Qd8+??  Rxd8  2.Rxd8+  Be8!  Black does not have to take on d8 now, and can block on e8 instead. White is now losing because of his material deficit. This brings me to the third element - exceptions. Exceptions are seemingly insignificant changes to a chess pattern that greatly alter the outcome of the tactic. This is a key weapon stronger players use to swindle average players. }
1... Rxa7 { Forcing Black to leave his b8 Rook unprotected } 2. Rd8+ Be8 3.
Rxb8
{ And White has just won a Rook. In sum, every chess pattern has its own unique clues, goals, and exceptions. To have a strong understanding of a given pattern, we will need to know all three. So without further ado, let’s practice applying PIE to simple patterns to improve our process of acquiring the three elements. }
