{ 1.	Notice how the relevant chess pieces interact. Take note of the responsibilities of every piece in making the tactic possible.  2.	Determine the types of strategical weaknesses in your opponent’s position that pave the way for the tactic.  3.	Consider changing the position slightly in a realistic manner. Check if the tactic still works, or if a different tactic becomes available.  4.	Mentally play out the tactical combination a few times. }
1. Ne7+ Kh8 2. Qxh7+ Kxh7 3. Rh3#
{ 1. Piece interactions and responsibilities:   @@StartBracket@@i@@EndBracket@@ White Knight covers g6 and g8   @@StartBracket@@ii@@EndBracket@@ White Queen opened h-file and forced 2…Kxh7   @@StartBracket@@iii@@EndBracket@@ White Rook delivers a ”side file checkmate”   @@StartBracket@@iv@@EndBracket@@ Black pawns and Rook, particularly the g7 pawn, prevented the King from escaping the mating net throughout this combination   2. Strategical weaknesses:   @@StartBracket@@i@@EndBracket@@ h7 must be unprotected at the starting position   @@StartBracket@@ii@@EndBracket@@ e7 must not be defended at the starting position   @@StartBracket@@iii@@EndBracket@@ Black must have poor control over the h-file and the h3 square   3a. Changes in the position that would make this particular combination fail:   @@StartBracket@@i@@EndBracket@@ Black has a c6 Knight   @@StartBracket@@ii@@EndBracket@@ Black has an f6 Knight   @@StartBracket@@iii@@EndBracket@@ Black Rook or two pawns are not on their current squares   @@StartBracket@@iv@@EndBracket@@ White Queen is replaced with a Bishop since 2. Bxh7+ can be met with 2…Kh8   @@StartBracket@@v@@EndBracket@@ Black has a Bishop on g7 instead of a pawn since 3. Rh3+ can be met with 3…Bh6   3b. Changes in the position that would still allow the tactic to work:   @@StartBracket@@i@@EndBracket@@ The White piece capturing h7 on move 2 can also be a Rook. For example, if White did not have the Queen but had another Rook on h3 }
