{ 1.	Notice how the relevant chess pieces interact. Take note of the responsibilities of every piece in making the tactic possible.  2.	Determine the types of strategical weaknesses in your opponent’s position that pave the way for the tactic.  3.	Consider changing the position slightly in a realistic manner. Check if the tactic still works, or if a different tactic becomes available.  4.	Mentally play out the tactical combination a few times. }
1. Qb3+ Kh8
{ 1...Kf8  2.Qf7  Be sure to keep key variations such as this one in mind for step 4 }
2. Nf7+ Kg8 3. Nh6+ Kh8 { 3...Kf8  4.Qf7 } 4. Qg8+ Rxg8 5. Nf7#
{ 1. Piece interaction and responsibilities:   (i)  White Queen prevented …Kf8 throughout the combination   (ii)  White Queen also forced 4…Rxg8   (iii)  White Knight helps the White Queen fulfil these two responsibilities   (iv)  White Knight delivers the checkmate by attacking a single square, h8   (v)  Black pawns and Rook smother their King to allow checkmate  2. Strategical weaknesses:   (i)  f7 must not be defended at the starting position   (ii)  Black must have poor control over the a2-g8 diagonal so that the initial check cannot be blocked  3a. Changes in the position that would make this particular combination fail:   (i)  Black pawns are not on their current squares   (ii)  Black h7 pawn is replaced with a Black Bishop since Qg8+ can be met with Bxg8  3b. Changes in the position that would still allow the tactic to work:   (i)  Black g7 pawn is replaced with a Black Bishop or a Knight   (ii)  Black has an additional Knight on e7 to cover g8   (iii)  Black Rook is on f8 instead of e8 since 1. Qb3+ Kh8 2. Nf7+ Rxf7 can be met with Qb8+ and a back rank mate }
