1. Qxc6+ bxc6 2. Ba6#
{ 1. Piece interaction and responsibilities:   (i)  White dark-squared Bishop prevents Kb8 and Kc7   (ii)  White light-squared Bishop delivers checkmate, taking away the two remaining squares, b7 and c8   (iii)  White Queen forces bxc6 which allows Ba6#   (iv)  Black Knight and Rook prevent the King from escaping the checkmate  2. Strategical weaknesses:   (i)  c6 must be unprotected at the starting position   (ii)  a6 must be unprotected at the starting position   (iii)  Black must have poor control over the f4-b8 diagonal  3a. Changes in the position that would make this particular combination fail:   (i)  Black Knight is replaced with a Black Bishop   (ii)  Black Knight or Rook are not on their current squares  3b. Changes in the position that would still allow the tactic to work:   (i)  Black c6 pawn is removed   (ii)  Black c6 pawn is replaced by any Black piece   (iii)  Black has an additional minor piece on a6   (iv)  Black pawn is on a6 instead of a7 }
