[Result "*"]
[FEN "r4rk1/p4ppp/Pp4n1/4BN2/1bq5/7Q/2P2PPP/3RR1K1 w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]

1. Qh6
{ Black has weak dark squares. Even though he has a g7 pawn, the attack from White's Knight and Bishop is too much to defend. On top of that, the Knight is within arm's reach of Ne7 and Nh6, which may remind you of the suffocation mate. All we need to do is remove the g7 from defending h6.   1.Bxg7  Bxe1  White may have an attack, but there is no immediate mate }
1... gxh6 { Otherwise, Qxg7 would be mate } 2. Nxh6# *

[Event "?"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "3. Pattern Internalisation Essentials"]
[Black "Applying PIE to Complex Patterns"]
[Result "*"]

{ PIE can be used at any time you see an unfamiliar tactical pattern, which can be found just about anywhere – from puzzle books and game analyses to chess live streams and Youtube videos. However, there are times you may find patterns which are so novel that you doubt you’ll ever see it in your own games. It is all to easy to dismiss them as one-offs which aren’t worthy to be internalised.  On the contrary, these combinations are gold mines that will give you the edge to outsmart your opponents. After all, if we only study common patterns, how will we ever see tactics that our opponents can’t?  Furthermore, studying complex and obscure combinations will significantly improve our tactical senses. For example, let’s suppose we have found a tactical shot emerging from a seemingly serene position. PIE will reveal the characteristics of that position which were precursors to the tactical combination. This enables us to evaluate positions more accurately, knowing whether a position is truly devoid of tactics.   With this in mind, we will practice applying PIE in tougher and more practical situations. Instead of giving familiar patterns and isolating them as seen in the earlier part of this chapter, we will be looking at 10 tactical shots taken from real games.   Again, I will list the steps of PIE at the start of every position and offer my observations along the way. Feel free to add on additional observations which I may not have included. At the end, I will test your knowledge with 5 relevant puzzles. }
1. -- *

[Event "?"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "3. Pattern Internalisation Essentials"]
[Black "Pattern #11 | Peter Svidler - Vladimir P Malaniuk"]
[Result "*"]
[FEN "r1bqr1k1/p4pp1/5n1p/3pb3/7B/2NB4/PP3PPP/R2Q1RK1 w - - 2 16"]
[SetUp "1"]

16. Nxd5
{ Svidler thought that he was winning a free pawn.  Switching move orders does not help White win the pawn   16.Bxf6  Qxf6  17.Nxd5  Qd6  Black will win at least the h2 pawn back }
16... Qxd5 17. Bxf6 Bb7
{ The key zwischenzug! Black is threatening mate on g2 while defending the Queen. White has to defend g2 in the next move. After which, Black will safely recapture on f6, remaining up a piece for just one pawn. Svidler immediately resigned here. Let's use PIE to understand what has just happened.   17...Bxf6??  18.Bh7+  Kxh7  19.Qxd5  This is the line Svidler had considered. However, Malaniuk was not obliged to recapture the f6 Bishop on the 17th move.   1. Piece interaction and responsibilities:   (i)  Black’s d8 Queen keeps an eye on d5.   (ii)  Black’s f6 Knight temporarily defends the Queen just before White plays Bxf6.   (iii)  Black’s c8 Bishop is ready to spring to life at b7, defending the Queen on d5 and threatening mate just in the nick of time.   (iv)  Black’s e8 Rook overprotects the e5 Bishop, which is attacked by the White f6 Bishop after move 17.  2. Strategical weaknesses:   (i)  White left g2 insufficiently defended.   (ii)  White’s h1-a8 diagonal is weak.  3a. Changes in the position that would make this particular combination fail:   (i)  If Black did not have the Rook on e8 defending the e5 Bishop. White will get away with the extra pawn after 16. Nxd5 Qxd5 17. Bxf6 Bb7 18. Bh7+ Kxh7 19. Qxd5 Bxd5 20. Bxe5    (ii)  If Black did not have a Bishop on e5. The reason the combination would not work is that White will have a way of saving g2 and his f6 Bishop simultaneously with 16. Nxd5 Qxd5 17. Bxf6 Bb7 18. Qg4! Hence, to add on to step 1, the e5 Bishop is important to attack the f6 Bishop in this line.   (iii)  If White had his Rook on e1. The e5 Bishop will no longer be overprotected and this becomes an issue after 16. Nxd5 Qxd5 17. Bxf6 Bb7 18. Bh7+ Kxh7 19. Qxd5 Bxd5 20. Bxe5   (iv)  If White had a Knight one move away from e3, such as f1. White will have 16. Nxd5 Qxd5 17. Bxf6 Bb7 18. Ne3 to simultaneously defend g2 and gain a valuable tempo on the d5 Queen, buying the time needed to save the f6 Bishop.  3b. Changes in the position that would still allow the tactic to work:   (i)  If White had a Knight one move away from f4. While a Knight can defend mate and attack the d5 Queen from f4, our e5 Bishop is watching that square. 16. Nxd5 Qxd5 17. Bxf6 Bb7 18. Nf4 Bxf4 }
*

[Event "?"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "3. Pattern Internalisation Essentials"]
[Black "Pattern #12 | Nihal Sarin - Einar Hjalti Jensson"]
[Result "*"]
[FEN "2rqr1k1/pb1nbp1p/1p2pnp1/2ppN3/3P1B2/2PBPN1Q/PP3PPP/3RK2R w K - 0 13"]
[SetUp "1"]

{ This game was played in the Runavik Open back in 2017 and remains one of Sarin's fastest victories. His opponent resigned immediately after 13. Nxf7 but let's take a look at what could have happened }
13. Nxf7
{ It is worthwhile noting that aside from attacking the d8 Queen, 13. Nxf7 comes with an auxiliary threat of 14. Bxg6 hxg6 15. Qh8+ Kxf7 16. Ng5# }
13... Kxf7 { 13...Qc7  14.Bxg6  hxg6  15.Qh8+  Kxf7  16.Ng5 } 14. Ng5+ Kg8 15.
Nxe6
{ 15.Qxe6+  Kg7  16.Qf7+  Kh8  17.Ne6   Let's now carry out PIE.   1. Piece interaction and responsibilities:   (i)  White e5 Knight attacks f7.   (ii)  White f3 Knight is prepared to attack f7 and e6 .   (iii)  White Queen attacks e6.   (iv)  Every Black piece around the Queen traps her.  2. Strategical weaknesses:   (i)  f7 is unprotected.   (ii)  e6 is unprotected.   (iii)  All of White’s minor pieces and the Queen are poised to attack the Kingside.   (iv)  Black is extremely cramped because he has less space and has too many pieces.  3a. Changes in the position that would make this particular combination fail:   (i)  If Black’s e8 Rook were on f8, protecting f7.   (ii)  If Black’s e7 Bishop were on f8, allowing the e8 Rook to defend e6.   (iii)  If White did not have his Knights on e5 and f3, or his Queen on h3.  3b. Changes in the position that would still allow the tactic to work:   (i)  If White did not have a Bishop on d3.   (ii)  If White did not have a Bishop on f4.   (iii)  If Black’s c8 Rook were on a8. Even though this un-traps the Black Queen, we can play 15. Qxe6+ with an attack instead of 15. Nxe6. }
*

[Event "?"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "3. Pattern Internalisation Essentials"]
[Black "Pattern #13 | Alexander Alekhine - Ronald MacKay Bruce"]
[Result "*"]
[FEN "rn1qkb1r/pp3p1b/2p1pnpp/4N3/2B4P/6N1/PPPPQPP1/R1B1K2R w KQkq - 2 11"]
[SetUp "1"]

{ This combination by the great Alekhine started from the Caro-Kann, Two Knights Attack. }
11. Nxf7
{ Forking the Queen and the Rook. It may seem that Black's best option is to take the Knight, but that leads to a forced mate.   1. Piece interaction and responsibilities:   (i)  White’s e5 Knight attacks f7.   (ii)  White’s c4 Bishop and e2 Queen attack e6.   (iii)  Black’s h6 pawn and h8 Rook trap the King. That said, even without those two pieces, White’s attack will still lead to victory but it is not as swift.  2. Strategical weaknesses:   (i)  f7 was unprotected.   (ii)  e6 was very poorly defended.   (iii)  Black’s light squares are extremely weak with the Bishop marooned at h7. It could have saved the day if it was defending either e6 or f7.  3a. Changes in the position that would make this particular combination fail:   (i)  If Black’s h7 Bishop were on a square that defends e6 or f7, such as c8 or g8.   (ii)  If Black’s Queen were on c7, overprotecting f7 .   (iii)  If Black’s f8 Bishop were on e7, since after 11. Nxf7 Kxf7 12. Qxe6+ Ke8, 13. Qf7+ is not checkmate.  3b. Changes in the position that would still allow the tactic to work:   (i)  If Black did not have a pawn on h6. This will provide an escape square for the King, but it is not a very good one. 11. Nxf7 Kxf7 12. Qxe6+ Kg7 13. Qf7+ Kh6 14. d3+ g5 15. hxg5# }
11... Kxf7 12. Qxe6+ Kg7 { Forced. } 13. Qf7# *

[Event "?"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "3. Pattern Internalisation Essentials"]
[Black "Pattern #14 | Wei Yi – Lazaro Bruzon"]
[Result "*"]
[FEN "3qr1k1/1b1rbp2/p2p1np1/1p2p3/4P3/P1NBB2Q/1PP3PP/4RR1K w - - 0 21"]
[SetUp "1"]

21. Nd5 { Removing the f6 Knight from its defensive outpost } 21... Nxd5
{ Now that the Knight is no longer defending the King, Wei Yi brilliantly crashes through without even bothering to recapture the Knight.   21...Bxd5  22.exd5  White will continue with Qf3, g4 and g5, and the pressure on f7 is tremendous. If Black plays Nxd5, White will carry out a similar attack to what was seen in the game.  22...Nxd5  23.Rxf7 }
22. Rxf7
{ Luring the King out for a walk. This sacrifice must be accepted because of the mate threat, Qh7# }
22... Kxf7
{ 22...Nf6  23.Qe6  Bf8  24.Re7+  Kh8  25.Qxf6+  Kg8  26.Qxg6+  Kh8  27.Qh7 }
23. Qh7+ Ke6 24. exd5+ Kxd5 { Black is threatening to run with Kc6-c7-b8 } 25.
Be4+ { The only way to keep the attack going } 25... Kxe4 26. Qf7
{ Taking away d5 } 26... Bf6 27. Bd2+ Kd4 28. Be3+ Ke4 29. Qb3
{ Threatening Qd3# while keeping an eye on the d5 escape square } 29... Kf5 30.
Rf1+ Kg4 31. Qd3 Bxg2+ 32. Kxg2 Qa8+ 33. Kg1 Bg5 34. Qe2+ Kh4 35. Bf2+ Kh3 36.
Be1
{ With a decisive threat of 37. Rf3+ Kg4 38. Qg2+ Kh5 39. Rh3+ Bh4 40. Rxh4#, Bruzon resigned here. This is certainly a long line, but we can still apply PIE. To clarify, these observations are made on the position just before 21. Nd5  1. Piece interaction and responsibilities:   (i)  White’s c3 Knight is in a position to challenge the f6 Knight.   (ii)  White’s f1 Rook has an X-ray attack on f7.   (iii)  White’s h3 Queen is attacking h7.   (iv)  All of White’s remaining pieces are well-centralised, and are ready for the king hunt after the Black monarch gets drawn out.  2. Strategical weaknesses:   (i)  Black’s pieces are not defending the King well. Only the f6 Knight is a good defender.   (ii)  Black has too many open lines towards the King.  3a. Changes in the position that would make this particular combination fail:  Given that all of White’s pieces are involved, changes in the position will likely alter the outcome of the attack. However, there are a few changes that will still allow the combination to work. These are listed in the next step.  3b. Changes in the position that would still allow the tactic to work:   (i)  If the e3 Bishop were on d2 or c1. In fact, the attack will be even more direct since the e1 Rook protects e4. When Black eventually plays 24…Kd5, we can respond with the same good move, 25. Be4+, where 25…Kxe4 is not possible.   (ii)  If the d3 Bishop were on e2 instead. After 23…Ke6, we have 24. Bg4+ and mate to follow. }
