[Event "?"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "3. Pattern Internalisation Essentials"]
[Black "Pattern #15 | Magnus Carlsen - Hikaru Nakamura"]
[Result "*"]
[FEN "3N4/kP6/P2p4/3Pp1p1/2KnPp2/5P2/5b2/8 w - - 0 59"]
[SetUp "1"]

{ This game was played in the 2018 chess.com Speed Chess Championship finals. Up a piece for two pawns, Nakamura was winning here. However, after careful play, Carlsen managed to equalise by pushing his two queenside pawns far up the board. }
59. Nf7
{ The beginning of a crafty 5-move manoeuvre. The Knight was headed for d7 and the only direct way for Nakamura to stop it was to play 59...Be1 followed by Ba5 }
59... Bh4
{ Nakamura overlooked Carlsen's idea   59...Be1  60.Nxd6  Ba5  61.Nc8+  Kb8  The Black Bishop controls b6 and the White Knight is so near, yet so far from d7. }
60. Nxd6 g4 61. Nc8+ Kb8 62. Nb6
{ Black cannot stop White from playing Nd7+ followed by b8=Q.   It's time to analyse it using PIE   1. Piece interaction and responsibilities:   (i)  White’s a6-b7 pawn duo mutually defend each other. The a6 pawn directly defends b7 while the b7 pawn threatens to Queen, preventing Kxa6.   (ii)  White’s Knight is the only piece on the board that can realistically control b8.   (iii)  White’s d5 pawn prevents Black from bringing another piece to the defense with …Nc6 or …Ne6.  2. Strategical weaknesses:   (i)  Black has allowed White to advance his passed pawns too far up the board.   (ii)  Black did not have enough defensive power. The lone King was no match for the two pawns and Knight.  3a. Changes in the position that would make this particular combination fail:   (i)  If the d5 pawn were removed. Black would easily play Nd4-c6 to stop the pawns.   (ii)  If the White King were on an unfortunate square  (such as a1)  that allows the Black Knight to give check while making its way to a defensive square. Had the White King been on a1, Black could play 60…Nb3+ and 61…Nc5, ready to capture on a6 with the Knight next. White would be just one move too slow in this scenario.  3b. Changes in the position that would still allow the tactic to work:   (i)  If the White King were on a less active square that did not allow any Knight checks, such as h1. After 61. Nc8+ Kb8, White will always have two ways to displace the Black King, c8-b6-d7 as shown in the game, and c8-e7-c6, both of which cannot be stopped. }
*

[Event "?"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "3. Pattern Internalisation Essentials"]
[Black "Pattern #16 | Anish Giri - Sam Shankland"]
[Result "*"]
[FEN "8/1p6/8/1P1k1K2/1P6/4B2n/8/8 w - - 0 45"]
[SetUp "1"]

45. b6
{ Shankland resigned here forgetting that this is a theoretical draw. To draw, Black's King just needs to shuffle between c8, d8 and d7 and White has no way of displacing it or attacking b7.  If the Black King could reach a8, that would also be a draw for the same reason.   45.b6  Kd6  46.Kg4  Kd7  47.Kxh3  Kc8  48.Bf4  Kd8  49.Kg4  Kd7  50.Kf5  Kc8  51.Ke6  Kd8  52.Kf7  Kc8  53.Ke7  Stalemate.  1. Piece interaction and responsibilities:   (i)  Black’s b7 pawn blocks the enemy pawns and sits on a light square, immune to the White dark-squared Bishop. Additionally, it also contributes to the stalemate resource.   (ii)  Black’s King prevents the White King from accessing b7 from c7 or c8   (iii)  White’s b6 pawn takes the c7 and a7 squares away from the Black King, contributing to the stalemate resource. Moreover, the b6 pawn prevents the White King from using the b6 square to attack b7.  2. Strategical weaknesses:   (i)  White has the Bishop on the wrong colour  (or Black has the pawn on the right colour)  .   (ii)  White’s b6 pawn occupied a square which the White King would want for itself. Moreover, that pawn also took away the a7 and c7 squares, helping Black stalemate itself.  3a. Changes in the position that would make this particular fortress fail:   (i)  If White’s b6 pawn were anywhere else.   (ii)  If White had a light-squared Bishop. White would simply sacrifice the Bishop for the b7 pawn and win the pawn endgame.   3b. Changes in the position that would still allow the fortress to work:   (i)  If Black had a couple of extra pawns on the Kingside. The excess reserve tempi do not change the fact that Black’s King cannot be displaced from guarding the b7 pawn. }
45... Ng1 *

[Event "?"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "3. Pattern Internalisation Essentials"]
[Black "Pattern #17 | Emanuel Lasker - Siegbert Tarrasch"]
[Result "*"]
[FEN "8/5KBB/p4b2/1pp1kb2/8/1P6/1P5P/8 b - - 0 37"]
[SetUp "1"]

Bxg7 38. Bxf5 Kxf5 39. Kxg7
{ Many would mistakenly think this pawn endgame favours Black. However, Lasker knew this was, in fact, a draw and went on to trade Bishops confidently. }
39... a5 40. h4 Kg4 41. Kg6
{ 41.Kf6??  Starting the race on f6, the King is one move short of catching the Queenside pawns  41...c4  42.bxc4  bxc4  43.Ke5  c3  44.bxc3  a4  45.Kd4  a3  46.Kd3  a2  47.Kc2  a1=Q }
41... Kxh4 42. Kf5 Kg3
{ This time, the King started his race from g6, which made all the difference   42...c4  43.bxc4  bxc4  44.Ke4  c3  45.bxc3  a4  46.Kd3  a3  47.Kc2  a2  48.Kb2 }
43. Ke4 Kf2 44. Kd5 Ke3 45. Kxc5 Kd3 46. Kxb5 Kc2 47. Kxa5 Kxb3
{ 1. Piece interaction and responsibilities:   (i)  White’s h-pawn bought White an extra move, which was used to play 41. Kg6   (ii)  White’s two Queenside pawns adequately held off Black’s majority.   2. Strategical weaknesses:  Not applicable  3a. Changes in the position that would make this particular combination fail:   (i)  If Black had just one extra move. For example, if his a6 pawn were to start on a5 instead.   (ii)  If Black had an extra pawn on c7. At first glance, it might seem that a pawn on c7 does not contribute to the King-and-pawn race, but it does help Black to consolidate his Queenside more easily. For example, he could play 39... b4 40. h4 c4 41. bxc4 Kg4 42. Kg6 Kxh4 43. Kf5 a5 44. Ke4 a4 45. Kd3 c5   3b. Changes in the position that would still allow the tactic to work:   (i)  If the Kings started a square to White’s left  (i.e. After move 39, White’s King on f7, and Black’s King on e5)  . Being closer to the Queenside and further from the Kingside can only benefit White. }
*

[Event "?"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "3. Pattern Internalisation Essentials"]
[Black "Pattern #18 | Alireza Firouzja - Giga Quparadze"]
[Result "*"]
[FEN "r4rn1/1pq3kp/p5p1/4pbQ1/2B1N3/2P2P2/PP3P2/2KR3R w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]

1. Rxh7+
{ Without this sacrifice, the h7 pawn will stave off White’s attack in several ways.  Not only is ...h5 a possibility to blunt the h-file, Black can also play Kh8 to let the c7 Queen laterally defend h7   1.Qh4  Kh8 }
1... Kxh7 2. Rh1+ Kg7 3. Qh4
{ With the h7 pawn gone, Black has neither Kh8 nor h5 to save him here } 3...
Rf7 { Trying to escape through f8 } 4. Qh8+ Kf8 5. Bxf7
{ Weakening the 7th rank to allow the White pieces to penetrate } 5... Qxf7 6.
Rh7 Qe6 7. Qg7+ Ke8 8. Nd6+ Qxd6 { 8...Kd8  9.Qc7 } 9. Qf7+ Kd8 10. Qxg8+ Qf8
11. Qxf8#
{ Now, let's carry out PIE.  1. Piece interaction and responsibilities:   (i)  One White Rook sacrificed itself for the h-pawn, paving the inroads for his brother.   (ii)  The other Rook was used to penetrate the newly opened h-file.   (iii)  The rest of White’s army was poised to attack the Black King.  2. Strategical weaknesses:   (i)  Black’s King hardly had any cover.   (ii)  Black’s already weak h-file was exacerbated by the g8 Knight, which prevented Black from defending with …Rh8  3a. Changes in the position that would make this particular combination fail:  Interestingly, Black’s position was so bad that this combination will still work in one form or another despite certain minute changes to the position. I will show a few examples in step 3b.  3b. Changes in the position that would still allow the tactic to work:  If the White Knight were on g4 instead of e4, we would have 1. Rxh7+ Kxh7 2. Rh1+ Kg7 3. Qh4 Rf7 4. Nxe5 where our Knight cannot be taken. 4…Qxe5 5. Qh8+ Kf8 6. Qxe5  If the White Knight were on f4 instead of e4, we can play 1. Rxh7+ Kxh7 2. Rh1+ Kg7 3. Nh5+ Kh8 4. Nf6+  If the White Knight were on d4 instead of e4, we can still play 1. Rxh7+ Kxh7 2. Rh1+ Kg7 3. Bxg8 Kxg8 4. Nxf5 Rxf5 5. Qxg6+ Qg7 6. Qxf5 with a decisive attack  It may seem that the Knight near the enemy King was key to the attack’s success. But what if Black had somehow managed to play …Bxe4 to get rid of the pesky Knight and White responded with fxe4  (The only changes from the initial position are that the e4 Knight and f5 Bishop are off the board and our f3 pawn is on e4)  ? We would need to play a preparatory move. 1. Rdg1 threatening Rxh7+ followed by Qxg6. Black might try 1. …Qd6 to defend g6 but comes 2. Rxh7+! Kxh7 3. Rh1+ Kg7 4. Qh4 Ne7 5. Qh6+ Kf6 6. Rh3 with a decisive attack, yet again. }
*

[Event "?"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "3. Pattern Internalisation Essentials"]
[Black "Pattern #19 |  Mikhail Tal - Vasily Smyslov"]
[Result "*"]
[FEN "r1bb1r1k/p4ppp/2p2n1N/7Q/2p5/2B2N2/qPP2PPP/2KR3R w - - 2 19"]
[SetUp "1"]

19. Qxf7
{ This sacrifice works despite the possible zwischenzug Queen checks Black has }
19... Qa1+
{ The Queen cannot be taken immediately because of back rank issues   19...Rxf7  20.Rxd8+  Rf8  21.Rxf8+  Ng8  22.Rxg8  Also, the Rook cannot run away because of the classic smothered mate pattern   19...Re8  20.Qg8+  Rxg8  21.Nf7 }
20. Kd2 { The White King now blocks his own Rook from moving to d8 } 20... Rxf7
{ 20...Qxd1+  This move is even worse than 20...Rxf7. Black will be down a full piece.  21.Rxd1  Rxf7  22.Nxf7+  Kg8  23.Nxd8 }
21. Nxf7+ Kg8 22. Rxa1 Kxf7
{ Smyslov resigned after a couple of moves. Let's execute PIE now.  1. Piece interaction and responsibilities:   (i)  White’s Queen attacked the f8 Rook and was prepared to sacrifice itself on g8 for a smothered mate.   (ii)  White’s h6 Knight aided the Queen in delivering the smothered mate. More importantly, it was ready to recapture on f7 with check.   (iii)  White’s d1 Rook was ready to deliver back rank mate on d8.  2. Strategical weaknesses:   (i)  Black did not defend his kingside sufficiently, in particular, the f7 square.   (ii)  Black had a weak back rank.   (iii)  d8 was unprotected.  3a. Changes in the position that would make this particular combination fail:   (i)  If Black’s back rank was more secure. For example, if the c8 Bishop were at b7, allowing the a8 Rook to defend d8.   (ii)  If f7 was sufficiently protected. For example, if Black had his a8 Rook on a7.  3b. Changes in the position that would still allow the tactic to work:   (i)  If White’s Knight were on e5 instead of h6. The only difference is that Black can play 19…Re8 and he is safe from a smothered mate, but White would still be winning in that variation.   (ii)  If White’s Knight were on g5 instead of h6. Black can, again, meet 19. Qxf7 with 19…Re8. But we have 20. Rhe1 to further add pressure to the back rank. Interestingly, the intuitive 19. Bxf6  (to remove the defender of h7)  does not work. Black will play 19…Bf5 20. Bc3 Qa4 to threaten Qxc2#. White’s attack will run out of steam while Black’s pieces spring to life because White spent too much time taking the f6 Knight and then retreating. }
*

[Event "?"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "3. Pattern Internalisation Essentials"]
[Black "Pattern #20 | Magnus Carlsen - Sergei Karjakin"]
[Result "*"]
[FEN "2R5/4bppk/1p1p4/5R1P/4PQ2/5P2/r4q1P/7K w - - 5 50"]
[SetUp "1"]

50. Qh6+
{ This famous mate in 2 was played in the final match of the rapid tiebreaks in the 2016 World Championship between Magnus Carlsen and Sergei Karjakin. Whichever way Black takes the Queen, White would still have mate }
50... gxh6 { 50...Kxh6  51.Rh8 } 51. Rxf7#
{ 1. Piece interaction and responsibilities:   (i)  White’s f5 Rook covered g5 and attacked f7.   (ii)  White’s c8 Rook covered the back rank and was ready to move to h8.   (iii)  White’s h5 pawn took away g6.   (iii)  White’s Queen either diverted the Black King from defending h8, or the g7 pawn from defending the 7th rank.  2. Strategical weaknesses:   (i)  White was dangerously active, with three major pieces and a far-advanced pawn near the Black King.   (ii)  Black had little defense – the e7 Bishop and the f7 and g7 pawns were ineffective in keeping the King safe.  3a. Changes in the position that would make this particular combination fail:   (i)  If Black somehow had f7 protected, such as having a Bishop on b3.   (ii)  If Black’s e7 Bishop were on f6, blocking the attack on f7.  3b. Changes in the position that would still allow the tactic to work:   (i)  If White did not have the h5 pawn – White would have 50. Rh5+ Kg6 51. Qf5#   (ii)  If Black had an extra pawn on h6. }
