{ This game was played at the Nutcracker Rapid 2017 between Vladislav Artimiev  (White)  and Shakhriyah Mamedyarov  (Black)  . }
13. Nxd5
{ Mamedyarov must have been familiar with the game between Peter Svidler and Vladimir P Malaniuk @@StartBracket@@Pattern #11@@EndBracket@@ and thought that the d5 pawn was taboo. However, there is a key difference in the position - Black does not have a Bishop on e5! Recall that we used PIE and concluded that a Black needed a Bishop on e5 to recapture on f6. }
13... Qxd5 14. Bxf6 Bb7 15. Qg4
{ Had the Black c5 Bishop been on e5, Black could have played Bxf6 here. Instead, Mamedyarov was forced to simplify into a worse endgame, from which Artemiev managed to squeeze a win. }
15... Qxg2+ 16. Qxg2 Bxg2+ 17. Kxg2
{ This rather recent example shows the importance of PIE @@StartBracket@@particularly the third step here@@EndBracket@@ in ensuring that we avoid getting rooked by such deceptively similar tactical patterns. }
