{ If the attacker's King is placed in front of the pawn  (referring to e4, f4, or g4 in this example)  , all is not lost. If we are able to take the opposition as the defender , the game is drawn. }
1. Kf2 Ke4 { Here, we must continue to maintain the opposition } 2. Ke2 Kf4 3.
Kf2 Kg4 4. Kg2
{ Eventually, Black will need to break the repetition, either by moving beside the pawn, or away from the pawn. Since we already know the drawing technique for the former, let's take a look at the latter. }
4... Kh4 5. Kf3 { Threatening to go after the pawn works best } 5... Kg5 6. Kg3
