{ In this position, we know that the plan is to move in front of our pawn, but how should we do it? Via the pawn’s left side or right side?   The answer lies in the enemy King's position. We want to chaperone the pawn from the OPPOSITE side of the enemy King. This is to avoid the Black King taking the opposition, or worse, stopping us from moving in front of our pawn altogether. }
1. Kd2
{ 1.Ke2  Kf5   1...Kf7  To show how impossible it is for White to win, I want to show that the ridiculous ...Kf7 still draws the game. The point is that Black can still take distant opposition, and then 'regular opposition' once we move in front of our pawn.  2.Ke3  Ke7!  3.Kd4  Kd6= }
1... Kf5 2. Kc3 Ke5 3. Kc4
{ Notice that the Black King is a tad too far from the drawing square, c6 }
3... Kd6 4. Kd4
{ Now, we are in front of our pawn and have the opposition. This secures the win. @@StartBracket@@Refer to Position 4@@EndBracket@@ }
