{ Now we will be examining untouchable pawns. These refer to a pair of pawns that a lone enemy King cannot capture because the two pawns mutually defend each other, either directly or indirectly. An example of this is connected pawns. These pawns are fearless in the face of a mere enemy King.   Despite the White King being far, this is a win because White can take his time to waltz his King up to help his pawns promote. And there is nothing the Black King can do about it in the meantime. The b6 pawn is directly protected by the a5 pawn, and the King can never touch the pawn on a5 because he will be leaving the pawn square of the b6 pawn  (Refer to Position 12)  . The following moves will clearly illustrate what I have described. }
1. Kf2 Ka6
{ Unable to capture the b6 pawn, the Black King goes for the one on a5 } 2. Ke3
{ The White King calmly makes his way over } 2... Kxa5
{ The Black King has got one of the pawns, but now the other one runs away } 3.
b7 *

