{ You may recall that when the attacker's King is in front of his pawn in K + P vs K endgames  (Refer to Positions 4 and 6)  , the position is winning. Likewise, this rule applies in K + R + P vs K + R endgames. Known as the Lucena Position, it can be won by using the bridge technique. First, we must allow the White King to step outside so that the pawn can promote. }
1. Rg1+ Kh7 2. Rg4
{ The White Rook now belongs precisely on the fourth rank. The variation with 2. Kf7 will explain why.  If the White King immediately steps outside, he will face an unstoppable barrage of checks   2.Kf7  Rf3+  3.Ke6  Re3+  4.Kd6  Rd3+  5.Kc5   5.Ke5  Re3+  Re3  This is a useful tip for rook endings: If there are THREE files/ranks between the checking Rook @@StartBracket@@Black@@EndBracket@@ and the enemy pawn @@StartBracket@@White@@EndBracket@@, the Rook can check the enemy King indefinitely. This is because the King is unable to attack the Rook while keeping his pawn defended. In this position, the furthest the White King can stray from his pawn is the fifth rank. Hence, the White Rook must provide cover on the fourth rank. }
2... Rd2 3. Kf7 Rf2+ 4. Ke6 Re2+ 5. Kf6 Rf2+ 6. Ke5 Re2+
{ With the Rook on the fourth rank, the White King does not need to step back to defend the pawn anymore. }
7. Re4 { And we promote next } *

