{ You may also remember that when the defender's King is in front of the attacker’s pawn in K + P vs K endgames  (Refer to Positions 2, 3, and 5)  , the position is drawn. This rule similarly applies in K + R + P vs K + R endgames.   Against Knight pawns or Rook pawns, the defensive technique is simple - place your Rook on the last rank and wait. A passive defence indeed. }
1. Rg1 Rf2 2. Rh1 Ra2 3. Rg1 Rb2+ 4. Ka1 Rc2 5. Kb1 Rb2+ 6. Ka1 Re2 7. Kb1 Ra2
8. Rh1
{ 8.Rh1  b2  If Black tries to win by threatening Ra1, he loses to  9.Rh3+  Kb4  10.Kxa2 }
*

