1. Rh1 { 1.Rc1  with the idea of Rc8 followed by Rb8 is also correct } 1... Ke7
2. Rh8 Kd6
{ Weaker is   2...Kd7  3.Rb8  Rc2  4.Kb7  Rb2+  5.Ka6  Ra2+  6.Kb6  Rb2+  7.Kc5  The Black King would prefer to be on d6 to trap the White King on the a and b-files }
3. Rb8 Ra2 4. Kb7 Rb2+ 5. Kc8
{ White chooses the only correct way to escape   5.Ka6  Ra2+  6.Kb6  Rb2+  7.Ka5  Ra2+  8.Kb6 }
5... Rc2+ 6. Kd8 Rh2 { Threatening Rh8# } 7. Rb6+ Kc5 8. Rc6+
{ 8.Ra6  Rh8+  The White King cannot step on the sixth rank because of the skewer. If the White King zigzags his way to the kingside on the last two ranks, Black will play Ra8 and quickly pick up the pawn with its King's help. }
8... Kd5
{ 8...Kb5  9.Rc8  Rh8+  10.Kc7  Rh7+  11.Kb8   8...Kxc6  9.a8=Q+  Even if the Rook and the King were not separated, this is an easy win  9...Kc5  10.Qc8+  Kd4  11.Qg4+  Kd5  12.Qf5+  Kc6  13.Qe6+  Kc5  14.Qe5+ }
9. Ra6
{ Only now is Ra6 strong because the Black King is no longer guarding the queenside }
9... Rh8+ 10. Kc7 Rh7+ 11. Kb6 Rh6+ 12. Kb5 *

