{ I will now present you yet another defensive idea in R+P vs R endings, the frontal attack.   In this position, it looks as though Black is about to convert this into a Lucena position  (Position 20)  as the Black King chaperones his pawn while the White king remains cut out. If the pawn had already crossed the fourth rank, the winning technique will be trivial. Otherwise, we do have a simple idea to hold the half point - constantly check the King or pressure the e-pawn. }
1. Rf1+ Ke4 2. Re1+ Kf4 3. Rf1+ Ke3 4. Re1+
{ This idea works because there are THREE ranks between the checking Rook and the enemy pawn. Knowing common endgame themes can truly help us solve theoretical endgames! }
4... Kf4 5. Rf1+ Ke4 6. Re1+ Kf5 7. Rf1+ Ke6 8. Re1 Rd4
{ Intending to block the checks with the Rook. This idea, however, fails as the Black Rook is now overworked on both axes. On top of that, he cannot advance his pawn as the Rook will be left undefended. }
9. Rf1 { Other waiting moves along the first rank are fine as well. } 9... Kd5
10. Ra1 { Still preventing ...e4 from being played. } 10... Ke4 11. Re1+ Kf5
12. Rf1+ Rf4
{ This move blocks the check, but allows the White King to go in front of the pawn. }
13. Rh1 e4 14. Kd2
{ White will soon convert this to a Philidor position @@StartBracket@@Refer to Position 22@@EndBracket@@ }
*

