{ Here is a deceptively similar position to the previous one  (Position 27)  . Let's see what happens if White decides to nonchalantly use the same winning idea here. }
1. Kd4 Rd8+ 2. Ke5 Re8+ 3. Kd5 Rd8+ 4. Kc6 Re8 5. Re1 Kg6 6. Kd7 Ra8
{ This is the key difference. With this much space on the side opposite to the Black King, Black is able to have THREE files between his rook and the white pawn. As seen in Position 23, this means that the defender is now capable of checking the attacker indefinitely. }
7. Rf1 Kg5 8. e5 Ra7+ 9. Kd6 Ra6+ 10. Kd5 Ra5+ 11. Kd6
{ White cannot stray too far from his pawn } 11... Ra6+ 12. Kc7 Kg6 13. Kd7
Ra7+ 14. Kc8 Re7 15. Re1 Kf5
{ It would seem that in this special case, two files is not enough for the win. However, there is a new feature in the position: The Black King is at the edge of the board, which allows for some mating ideas. We'll find out how to use that resource in the next position. }
