[Round "?"]
[White "4. Essential Theoretical Endgames"]
[Black "Position 34 | K+R+P vs K+R #15"]
[Result "*"]
[FEN "R7/6k1/P7/8/8/8/r7/6K1 w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]

{ What if the pawn were on the sixth rank instead of the seventh? What difference does it make? Let's find out. }
1. Kf1 Kf6 2. Ke1 Kf7 3. Kd1 Ra1+ 4. Kc2 Ra2+ 5. Kb3 Ra1 6. Kc4 Kg7 7. Kb5 Kf6
8. Kb6 Rb1+ 9. Ka7
{ This is White's key idea. With the pawn on a6, a7 serves as a pocket where the King can hide from the perpetual checks. This position is now winning. }
9... Ke6 10. Rb8 Ra1 11. Rb6+ Kd7 12. Kb7 { With a7 and a8=Q to follow } *

[Event "?"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "4. Essential Theoretical Endgames"]
[Black "Position 35 | K+R+P vs K+R #16"]
[Result "*"]
[FEN "R7/8/5k2/8/8/p7/6K1/r7 w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]

{ If the pawn is on the sixth rank, the way to draw is by checking and pressuring the pawn from the side, where the a7 pocket will be ineffective. This is known as the Vancura Defence. }
1. Rf8+ { Transferring to f3 } 1... Ke5 2. Rf3
{ We have completed the setup for the Vancura Defence.  From here on, there are only four things to remember:   1. If you can give safely a check to the enemy King from the side, go for it. @@StartBracket@@An example of an unsafe side check is shown on move 8@@EndBracket@@   2. Otherwise, keep your Rook on the sixth rank to pressure the pawn from the side.    3. If the pawn ever moves to the seventh rank, play Ra3 and return to the drawing idea shown in Position 33.   4. If Black ever plays Ra2+, don't move your King to the promotion rank. }
2... Ke4 3. Rb3 Kd4 4. Rf3 Kc4 5. Rf4+ Kc5 6. Rf5+ Kd6 7. Rf6+ Kd5 8. Rf3
{ Rf5 is not a safe check because the Black King will approach the White Rook, preventing Rf3 and any additional side checks.   8.Rf5+??  Ke4  9.Rf3  Rg1+  10.Kf2  Rf1+  11.Kxf1  Kxf3 }
8... Kc4 9. Rf4+ Kd5 10. Rf3
{ Similar to the 8th move, this is the only way to draw. } 10... Ra2+ 11. Kg3 *

