{ I have included this position to highlight the importance of rule 4 in the Vancura position  (Position 35)  : If the attacker gives a check on the second rank, the defender's King must avoid moving to the promotion rank. }
1. Ra7+
{ In this position, Black's King must steer clear of the last rank. Let's find out what happens if Black thoughtlessly plays ...Kg8?? }
1... Kg6
{ This is the only move that draws. It keeps the King centralised while remaining within arms reach of the 'safe zone'   1...Kg8??  2.Rb7  Ra5  3.a7  White's threat of Rb8+ followed by a8=Q is unstoppable  3...Kf8  4.Rb8+  Ke7  5.a8=Q  Rxa8  6.Rxa8   1...Kf6  2.Kd4  Ra5   2...Rc6  3.Kd5  Re6  4.Rh7!  This is why the Black King is better off on g6 rather than f6.  3.Ra8  Kg7  4.Kc4  Ra1  5.Kb5  White will use the a7 pocket to win the game @@StartBracket@@Refer to Position 34@@EndBracket@@.   1...Kh6??  Now, Black is severely disadvantaged in the King race to the queenside  2.Rb7  Ra5  3.a7  Kg6  4.Kd4  Kf6  5.Kc4  Ke6  6.Kb4  Ra1  7.Kc5  Black is one move short of drawing the game. Here, the White King will make its way to the b8 square and queen the pawn. }
2. Rb7
{ 2.Kd4  Rc6  3.Kd5  Rf6  We return to the Vancura set up @@StartBracket@@Position 35@@EndBracket@@ }
2... Ra5 3. a7 Kf6 4. Kd4 Ke6 5. Kc4 Kd6 6. Kb4 Ra1 7. Kb5 Rb1+
{ The game will end in perpetual check } 8. Ka6 Ra1+ 9. Kb6 Rb1+ 10. Ka6 Ra1+
11. Kb6 Rb1+ *

