{ In a Queen endgame, if the defender's King is in front of the attacker's pawn. It is nearly always a draw. This is because the defender often has the option to trade queens into a drawn King and pawn ending  (Refer to Positions 2, 3 and 5) }
1. Kd2 Qb2+ 2. Kd3 Qb3+ 3. Kd2 Qb2+ 4. Kd3 Qd4+ 5. Ke2 Kc4 6. Qf3 Qb2+ 7. Kd1
{ White is prepared to meet 7...d4 with 8. Qc6+ } 7... Qb1+
{ 7...d4  8.Qc6+  Kd3  9.Qf3+  Kc4 } 8. Kd2
{ It is worthwhile noting that   8.Ke2  loses the game. This is because the King is going away from the front of the pawn.  8...Qc2+  forcing a favourable Queen trade  9.Kf1   9.Ke1  Qc3+  Qd3+ }
8... Qb2+ 9. Kd1 Qd4+ 10. Kc2
{ The checks end and now White will have all the fun drawing this position with probably Qb3+ next. }
