[SetUp "1"]

{ Let’s now consider Queen + Knight pawn vs Queen or Queen + Rook pawn vs Queen endgames. If the defender CANNOT put his King in front of the pawn, the attacker has some chance of winning. To maximise this chance, the attacker should try to place his King on the same file/rank as the defender's. Why? Doing so will set up the possibility of pins and counter-checks as the attacker's Queen blocks the checks. When these counter-checks happen, a queen trade is forced and the attacker will win the resulting pawn endgame.   If you are the defender, you should should try to run your King to the diametrically opposite corner from the pawn's queening square. That is the ideal corner for the defender’s King. I will first illustrate this defensive idea through a negative example. Here, the Black King is miles away from his ideal corner. The attacker, White, can try to make use of Black's poor king position to set up a counter-check. }
1. Kh7 Qe4 2. Qc5
{ In addition to centralising the Queen, this move stops the King from running to the safe corner, the squares around a1. }
2... Qh1+ 3. Kg7 Qb7+ 4. Kf6 Qf3+ 5. Qf5 Qc3+ 6. Qe5 Qf3+ 7. Ke7 Qb7+ 8. Ke8
Qc8+ 9. Kf7 Qb7+ 10. Qe7 { Black has run out of good checks } 10... Qb5
{ 10...Qf3+  11.Qf6+  Qxf6+  12.Kxf6   10...Qb3+  11.Qe6+  Qxe6+  12.Kxe6 } 11.
g7 Qh5+ 12. Kf6 Qh4+ 13. Ke6 Qg4+ 14. Ke5 Qh5+ 15. Kf4
{ This engine move is difficult to understand. After some probing, I found that it was played because it avoids Black's many diagonal checks that prevents the White Queen from ever getting any counter-checks   15.Ke6  Qg4+  16.Kd6  Qb4+  17.Kd7  Qa4+ }
15... Qh2+ 16. Kf5 Qh3+ 17. Kg6 Qg4+ 18. Qg5 Qe4+ 19. Qf5 Qe8+ 20. Kh7 Qe7 21.
Qd3+ Ka7 22. Qd4+ { controlling h4 } 22... Ka6 23. Kh8 Qe6 { The last trick }
24. Qd3+ { 24.g8=Q??  Qh6+  25.Qh7  Qf8+ } 24... Ka5 25. Qd8+
{ Controlling f6 and f8 } 25... Ka4 26. g8=Q Qh6+ 27. Qh7
{ Black is out of checks } *

