{ We will now move on to endgames where the attacker has a Queen and the defender has a pawn on the brink of promotion.   The attacker's plan is to use the Queen to check the King until he is forced in front of his own pawn. That makes the pawn  unable to promote on the subsequent move, and the attacker has a free move to inch his King closer to the battle scene.   The evaluations of such endgames depend on which file the pawn is located. If the pawn is on the d- or e-file, the attacker can successfully carry out the plan and will win. }
1. Qd7+
{ The Queen starts zigzagging her way towards the pawn, ensuring that every move comes with check. }
1... Kc1
{ Black's King will try to keep clear of the promotion square. He threatens to promote now, not giving White any time to move the White King over yet. }
2. Qe6 { Stopping promotion while threatening to take the pawn. } 2... Kd1 3.
Qd5+ Kc1 4. Qe4 Kd2 5. Qd4+ Kc1 6. Qe3+ Kd1 7. Qd3+
{ After 7 moves, White finally gives the perfect check that forces Black's King to move in front of his pawn }
7... Ke1 { 7...Kc1  8.Qxe2 } 8. Kg7
{ Of course, White uses this free move to bring the King over. } 8... Kf2 9.
Qd2
{ White now repeats the process of forcing the King in front of the pawn. This will take another four moves or so. }
9... Kf1 10. Qf4+ Kg2 11. Qe3 Kf1 12. Qf3+ Ke1 13. Kf6
{ Yet again, White uses his free move to bring the King over. } 13... Kd2 14.
Qf2 Kd1 15. Qd4+ Kc1 16. Qe3+ Kd1 17. Qd3+ Ke1 18. Ke5 Kf1
{ Walking into a pin. This move does not threaten to promote. } 19. Kf4 Kf2 20.
Qf3+ Ke1 21. Ke3 Kd1 22. Qxe2+ { And the rest is elementary } *

