{ Opposite coloured Bishop endgames have very high drawing tendencies, and this position illustrates that clearly.  Even though Black is up three pawns here, the opposite coloured Bishops and the poor pawn position makes this a draw. This is because Black cannot push his pawns any further. White has 2 pieces controlling the squares in front of the pawns whereas Black can have at most one piece  (i.e. his king)  controlling those squares. If some of the pawns had been on light squares, they could support one another to make progress. Unfortunately for Black, this is not the case. The only thing White needs to do to hold the draw is ensure that his own King follows the enemy King. }
1. Bd5 Kf6 2. Be4 Ke6 3. Bb7 Kd6
{ Evidently, Black is trying to make progress with his King on the queenside. White must move his King over to combat that. }
4. Ke2
{ 4.Ke4??  g2   4.Be4??  White cannot sit around passively either  4...Kc5  5.Bb7  Kd4  6.Bc6  e4+!  7.Bxe4   7.Kxf4  g2  g2!  8.Kxg2  Kxe4 }
4... Kc5 5. Kd3 { Stopping the Black King in its tracks } 5... Kd6 6. Ke2 Ke6
7. Kf3 Kf5 8. Be4+ Kg5 9. Bd5 Kh4
{ Now that Black is headed for the Kingside, White must defend against the invasion. }
10. Kg2 { 10.Bc6??  Kh3  Black will play g2 and win the Bishop next. } 10...
Kg4 11. Be4 *

