{ If Black defends the e6 square with ...Bb3, f5 is not under any pressure. White's King will be free to move. }
1. Bg5+
{ White begins with a probing check, whilst controlling the e7 square  For greater clarity, read the main variations before looking at this one.   1.Bb4+?  This is the wrong way to give a check because it does not control f6  1...Kf7  2.Kd4  Bc2  Normally we would have the e6-f6 combo. However...  3.e6+  Kf6  4.e7  Ba4  Draw }
1... Kf7
{ Whichever way the Black King runs to, White will use his King to assist the e-pawn from the other side.   1...Kd7  2.Kf4  Bc4  3.Bh4  Bf7  4.Kg5  Ke7  5.Kh6+  Kd7  6.Kg7  Bb3  7.Kf6  e6 next and White wins }
2. Kd4 Ba2
{ 2...Bc2  3.e6+  then f6 next. Black cannot play 3. ...Kf6. This is why we chose Bg5+ for our probing check as opposed to Bb4+ }
3. Kc5 Bb3 4. Kd6 { e6 next and White wins } *

