[SetUp "1"]

{ Given that a 2-pawn advantage is often decisive in same coloured Bishop endings, I will be covering positions where the attacker has only one extra pawn.   As with many other simplified endings, if the defender's King stands in front of the enemy pawn  (on a square opposite to the bishop's colour)  , the draw is obvious. This is because the defender can simply trade Bishops into a drawn ending. }
1. Bc5 Be7 2. Bxe7 Kxe7 3. Kf5 Ke8
{ This is an easy draw @@StartBracket@@Refer to Position 2@@EndBracket@@ } *

[Event "?"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "4. Essential Theoretical Endgames"]
[Black "Position 54 | Same Coloured Bishops #2"]
[Result "*"]
[FEN "8/8/8/8/7b/3p1k2/3B4/5K2 w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]

{ Let's analyse this ending where the defender's King cannot go in front of the pawn. }
1. Bh6 Ke4 2. Kg2
{ First, recognise that a lone Bishop is no match against a King, Bishop and pawn. Passive play, as seen in the 2. Bd2 variation, leads to a loss. Instead, as the Black King rushes to c2, the White King must, likewise, make a run for the c4 square.   2.Bd2  Kd5  3.Ba5  Kc4  4.Bd2  Kb3  5.Ba5  Kc2  6.Bb4  Bf6  7.Ke1  Bc3+  8.Bxc3  Kxc3  9.Kd1  d2  10.Ke2  Kc2 }
2... Kd4 3. Kf3 Kc3 4. Ke4 Kc2 5. Kd5 Be1 6. Kc4
{ By taking opposition from the rear, Black will never be able to play ...Bc3 to challenge White’s Bishop on the a5-e1 diagonal. }
6... Bd2 7. Bf8 Bf4 8. Bb4 Be5
{ If the White King had been far away, the threat of 9...Bc3 would be decisive. Thankfully for White, he's got that under control. }
9. Ba5 Bf4 10. Bc3 Bd2
{ Chasing the White Bishop from the a5-e1 diagonal by playing Bd2 is ineffective because it blocks the pawn. }
11. Bg7 Ba5
{ If the White Bishop makes way for ...d2 via the a5-e1 diagonal, we will use the c1-h6 diagonal.   11...Bg5  If the Black Bishop makes way for ...d2 via the c1-h6 diagonal, we will use the a5-e1 diagonal. }
12. Bh6
{ To sum this idea up, if the defender's King cannot reach the front of the pawn, he should try to stay behind the enemy King instead. }
*

