[FEN "2KB4/1P6/2k5/8/8/8/7b/8 w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]

{ There is an exception to the rule mentioned on the previous position  (Position 50)  . Here, even though Black has optimally taken opposition from behind, the position is lost for him. This is because there is only one diagonal from which the Black Bishop can defend the queening square, the h2-b8 diagonal. The a7-b8 diagonal is too short. }
1. Bh4 Kb5 2. Bf2 Ka6 3. Bc5
{ The only move that makes progress. Other waiting moves with the Bishop allow Bd6, and we will see why that is a problem.   3.Be3  Bd6  4.Bg5  Kb5  5.Bd8  Kc6  6.Be7  Bh2  White cannot transfer to the g1-a7 diagonal }
3... Bg3 4. Be7 { Threatening Bd8-Bc7 } 4... Kb5 5. Bd8 Kc6 6. Bh4
{ Obtaining a key tempo that would not have been possible with the Bishop at h2 or d6 @@StartBracket@@because after the Bishop moves, White can't transfer to the g1-a7 diagonal with Bc5@@EndBracket@@ }
6... Bh2 7. Bf2 Kb5 8. Ba7 Ka6 9. Bb8 Bg1 10. Bg3 Ba7 11. Bf2
{ Again, the a7-b8 diagonal is too short for the defence to hold. } *

[Event "?"]
[Site "?"]
