[Round "?"]
[White "4. Essential Theoretical Endgames"]
[Black "Position 56 | N+P vs K+P #1"]
[Result "*"]
[FEN "K7/8/8/8/2N2p2/5P2/4k3/8 w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]

{ We will now look at endgames where the attacker has an extra Knight, and a pawn blockaded by an enemy pawn. Even if the attacker's King is far away, the position is a win. Here, as long as the White Knight defends the pawn from e5 or g5, he will bring home the full point. This is because the Black King cannot attack the Knight and pawn simultaneously. }
1. Ne5 Ke3
{ As Black tries to attack the Knight in vain, White brings his King over. } 2.
Kb7 Kd4 3. Nf7 Ke3
{ Black can hope for a repetition here, but White can defend the pawn from the other good square. }
4. Ng5 { The Black King will take ages to reach the Knight now. } 4... Kd4 5.
Kc6
{ White's King will pick up the f4 pawn, and he will enlist the help of the Knight if needed. }
*

[Event "?"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "4. Essential Theoretical Endgames"]
[Black "Position 57 | N+P vs K+P #2"]
[Result "*"]
[FEN "8/8/2K5/p7/P7/1n6/8/7k w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]

{ If the blockaded pawns are on a side file, the game is a draw. The attacker's Knight will only have one good square. In this case, the square is b3 }
1. Kb5 Kg2 2. Kc4 Nd2+ 3. Kb5 Nb3 4. Kc4 Na1 5. Kb5
{ The Knight would really love to move to the square to the left of a3, but that is not legal here. }
5... Nb3 6. Kc4 *

[Event "?"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "4. Essential Theoretical Endgames"]
[Black "Position 58 | K+R vs K"]
[Result "*"]
[FEN "8/3k4/7R/4K3/8/8/8/8 w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]

{ Let's briefly take a look at the single Rook checkmating technique. I included this position certainly not to teach the Rook checkmate, but to demonstrate how the attacker's King chases the defender's. This will give you a deeper appreciation of the two Bishop checkmate. }
1. Rg6
{ As the attacker, we do NOT want to take opposition. Instead, we want Black to take the opposition with 1. ...Ke7, so that we can play Rg7+, pushing him to the last rank. }
1... Kc7
{ Black does not want to play ...Ke7 and get forced back, nor does he want to voluntarily walk to the last rank. This move puts up the most resistance.   1...Ke7  2.Rg7+ }
2. Kd5 Kb7 3. Kc5 { The chase continues } 3... Ka7 4. Rg7+ Kb8
{ 4...Ka6  Here, White just needs to make a waiting move with the Rook along the 7th rank.  5.Rc7  Black's King is now forced to take the opposition and we have checkmate.  5...Ka5  6.Ra7 }
5. Kc6 { Not taking opposition, but instead goading Black to do so } 5... Ka8
{ 5...Kc8  6.Rg8 } 6. Kb6 Kb8 7. Rg8#
{ With this idea of chasing the defender's King in mind, let's move on to the two Bishop checkmate }
*

[Event "?"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "4. Essential Theoretical Endgames"]
[Black "Position 59 | K+2B vs K"]
[Result "*"]
[FEN "8/8/8/4k3/8/8/8/2B1KB2 w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]

{ The first thing White should do is create a barrier by keeping his two Bishops on adjacent diagonals. }
1. Bd3 Kd4 2. Ke2 Ke5 3. Be3 Kd5
{ Now that the barrier has been created, the Bishops will work with the White King to slowly force the Black King to the corner. }
4. Kd2 Ke5 5. Bf1 Ke4 6. Bg2+ Kf5 7. Bf2 Kf4 8. Kd3 Ke5 9. Bg3+ Ke6
{ Without the White King on d3, Black would have played ...Kd4 instead. } 10.
Kd4
{ With d5 under control, White threatens Bh3+ to push the Black King to the corner. }
10... Kd7 11. Kc5 { Now White controls c6, threatening Bh3+ yet again. } 11...
Kc8 12. Kb6 { The chase ends here. White will finally get Bh3+ next. } 12...
Kd7 13. Bh3+ Ke7 14. Kc5 Kf6 15. Kd4 Kg5 16. Ke4 Kf6 17. Bh4+ Kg6 18. Bg4 Kh6
19. Kf5 Kh7 20. Bh5 Kh6 21. Bg6 Kg7 22. Bf6+ Kg8 23. Bg5 Kf8 24. Kf6 Kg8 25.
Bh6 Kh8 26. Bf5 Kg8 27. Kg6
{ The White King and dark-squared Bishop alone put the King in a two-square prison, g8 and h8. It is time to take away both squares, one at a time. }
27... Kh8 28. Bg7+ { Taking away h8 } 28... Kg8 29. Be6#
{ And then taking away g8 with checkmate } *

[Event "?"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "4. Essential Theoretical Endgames"]
[Black "Position 60 | K+B+N vs K"]
[Result "*"]
[FEN "8/8/8/4k3/8/8/8/4KBN1 w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]

{ Before we explore the position, there are three ideas to remember:   1. Checkmate can only be forced in a corner of the same colour complex as your Bishop. For example, if you have a dark-squared Bishop, you can only force checkmate at a dark-squared corner.   2. Waiting moves are sometimes necessary, and should only be done by the King or the Bishop, never by the Knight.   3. The Bishop and Knight will coordinate best when the Knight is on a square of the same colour complex as the Bishop. For example, if you have a light-squared Bishop, the Knight is best placed on a light square.   Now that you know the general ideas, let's check out the specific technique. Step 1: Control the centre }
1. Bd3 Kd4 2. Ke2 Ke5 3. Ke3 Kd5 4. Be4+ Kc5 5. Kd3 Kb4 6. Kd4 Kb3
{ Black cannot keep his King in the middle now that White is occupying the centre. The next best option is run to the dark squared corner where mate cannot be forced }
7. Ne2 Ka2 8. Kc3 Ka1 9. Nd4 Ka2 10. Nc2
{ The Knight is the only piece that can force the king out of a1. } 10... Kb1
11. Kb3 Kc1 12. Nd4 Kd2
{ Notice how the last three moves by White have pushed the enemy King to the checkmating corner. }
13. Ne6 Ke3 14. Bg6
{ Thanks to the Bishop and Knight both working on light squares, White has achieved perfect coordination. The White Knight, Bishop, and King work together to form a prison of 17 squares }
14... Kd2 15. Kb2 Kd1 16. Bd3
{ White wants to play Bc2 only after ...Kd2 to force the enemy back, hence this waiting move }
16... Kd2 17. Bc2 Ke2 18. Kc1 Ke3 19. Kd1 Kf3 20. Kd2 Kg4 21. Bg6
{ Covering h5 } 21... Kf3 22. Kd3 Kg4 { Other moves allow Bh5 } 23. Ke3 Kg3 24.
Bh5 Kh4 25. Be2
{ Now that the Bishop has improved its position, the Knight must follow suit }
25... Kg3 26. Ng5 Kh4 27. Ne4
{ The Knight and the Bishop alone trap the King in a prison of 6 squares. The White King is free to further restrict the movement of the enemy. }
27... Kh3 28. Kf4 Kh4 29. Kf5 Kh3 30. Kg5 Kg2 31. Kh4 Kh2 32. Bf1 Kg1 33. Bh3
Kh2 34. Nc3 Kg1 35. Kg3
{ Similar to what we saw in the two Bishop checkmate, the King and light-squared Bishop alone trap the enemy King in a prison of two squares, g1 and h1. White will now take both squares away, one at a time. }
35... Kh1 36. Bg2+ { Taking away h1 } 36... Kg1 37. Ne2#
{ Taking away g1 with mate } *

[Event "?"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "4. Essential Theoretical Endgames"]
[Black "Chapter Conclusion"]
[Result "*"]

{ Here are some of the general principles from the 60 positions we can infer.  1. As the attacker, you’d much rather have a central pawn than a flank pawn.   This is often true whether the endgames involves Rooks, Queens, or even no pieces. One of the few exceptions is when the attacker has a rook pawn and a King vs a lone Knight  (Refer to Position 47)  .  2. If the defender’s King is in front of the attacker’s pawn, drawing chances significantly increase.  Again, this can be observed whether in endgames with Rooks, Queens, Bishops, or in pure pawn endgames. The reason is simple. The defender has the option to trade down to a drawn pawn endgame.  Refer to Positions 2, 3, 5, 21, 22, 37, and 53   3. There must be at least THREE files/ranks between a checking Rook and the enemy pawn for perpetual check.  I am sure you’ve seen this one many times throughout this chapter  (Refer to Positions 20, 23, 24, 26, 28, 31 and 32)  .  4. In opposite coloured Bishop endings, if the attacker’s pawns are on the opposite colour complex to the defender’s Bishop, drawing chances greatly increase.   (Refer to position 49)   5. In same coloured Bishop endings where the attacker has a single extra pawn, if the defender cannot bring his King in front of the pawn, his best chance is to take opposition from the rear.    (Refer to Position 54)   6. Knights perform best in the centre where they have the most options.  The Knight’s lack of space really shows when it is pit against a King and a pawn on the seventh rank. Against central pawns, the Knight has more space and is able to draw  (Refer to Position 45)  . However, against rook pawns, the Knight is cramped and cannot hold the fort  (Refer to Position 47)  . Unsurprisingly, the only exception to this is if the rook pawn were on the sixth rank  (or closer to the centre)  , where the Knight has ample room to fend off the King and the pawn  (Refer to Position 48)  .  7. The Bishop and Knight will coordinate best when the Knight is on a square of the same colour complex as the Bishop.   This coordination is evident in the Bishop and Knight checkmate  (Refer to position 60)  . }
1. -- *

