{ In the first part of this chapter on fortresses, schematic thinking was implicitly introduced. Recall that we had to determine the result of a few positions. Instead of calculating every possible 50-move variation to check if the position was drawn, we needed to assess only the attacker’s critical plans. Such is the beauty of schematic thinking.  Schematic Thinking is thinking in terms of plans rather than specific moves. The idea is not to be bogged down with many variations initially. Once we have a sense of how the game should be steered, we can delve deeper by working on candidate moves.  Likewise, when approaching other types of endgames, we should first identify the long-term goals. These include a potential configuration of pieces, freeing one of our pieces, or resolving a weakness. These long-term goals in themselves can serve as new tactical motifs we can work with. We can then consider candidate moves based on these motifs. Now let’s practice schematic thinking with a couple of puzzles. }
1. -- *

[Event "?"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "5. Endgame Ideas"]
[Black "Puzzle #3"]
[Result "*"]
[FEN "K1k5/4n3/8/8/8/8/P1P5/8 b - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]

{ What is the end position we are aiming for and how do we work towards it? }
1... Nc6
{ The plan here is to trap the White King in the corner and wait with Kc7-Kc8-Kc7-Kc8. After zugzwang eventually forces White to push his a2 pawn to a7, we will reroute our Knight to b6 or c7 to deliver checkmate.   White's only hope is stalemate but his c-pawn will still have a couple of legal moves. Hence, White's goal is to push the c-pawn as far as it can  (to c5)  before pushing his a-pawn.   Let’s consider a likely scenario a few moves later: It is Black to play, White’s pawns are on c5 and a7 while our King is on c8 and our Knight is still on c6. We can play Nc6-Nb4-Nd5-Nb6# in the time it takes for White to play c5-c6-c7. However, if Black’s King were on c7 instead of c8, the pawn would be stopped on the c6 square and White will get his stalemate. Can White achieve this position where our King is on c7 instead of c8? Yes. This is because he has the option to lose/gain tempi by starting with either c3 or c4. Black does not have this luxury. The King must strictly oscillate between c7 and c8.  This means we need to start our Knight reroute a move earlier when White’s pawn is on a6. This position can be seen after White’s 6 move in the main variation. }
2. c4 Kc7 3. a4 Kc8 4. c5 Kc7 5. a5 Kc8 6. a6
{ If our King moves to c7, White's pawn will be locked at a6 and he will escape thanks to stalemate. We must start our Knight reroute now. }
6... Nd4
{ 6...Nb4??  7.Ka7  Nd5  8.c6  Kc7  9.Ka8  Aside from heading for either c7 OR b6, the Black Knight must also control the a7 square to prevent Ka7 }
7. Ka7 Kc7 { We must stop Kb6 } 8. c6
{ 8.Ka8  Nb5  9.c6  This transposes to the main variation  9...Kc8  10.c7  Nxc7+  11.Ka7  Nd5  12.Ka8  Kc7  13.Ka7  Ne7  14.Ka8  Nc8  15.a7  Nb6 }
8... Nb5+ 9. Ka8 Kc8 10. c7 Nxc7+ 11. Ka7 Nd5 { Again, we must stop Kb6 } 12.
Ka8
{ Now our next goal is to control a7 with our Knight while preparing for either Nb6# OR Nc7#. The two candidates are b5 and c8. At the same time, our King should be on c7 to prevent Kb6. That means Nd5-Nc3-Nb5-Nc7# will not be possible. Instead, we should go for Nd5-Ne7-Nc8-Nb6# }
12... Kc7
{ 12...Nc3??  Heading for c7 via b5 is not possible. We don't have enough time  13.a7   13.Ka7  Even if White played Ka7, we cannot win with the Nb5-Nc7# plan  13...Kc7  14.Ka8  Nb5  15.a7  If only c7 were vacant }
13. Ka7 Ne7 14. Ka8 Nc8 15. a7 Nb6# *

[Event "?"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "5. Endgame Ideas"]
[Black "Puzzle #4"]
[Result "*"]
[FEN "3n4/7p/6p1/2kp2P1/1P3P2/4K3/4B3/8 b - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]

{ This is a game between Maxime Vachier-Lagrave  (White)  and Sam Shankland  (Black)  . What are some possible goals and how do we work towards them? }
1... Kxb4 2. Kd4 Nc6+ 3. Kxd5 Kc3
{ The only way White can win is by promoting the two pawns. They are currently fixed on dark squares. This means they cannot be defended by the light-squared Bishop. Therefore, the only goal is to activate our King and attack those pawns. Nothing else matters, not even our Knight. }
4. Kxc6 { The c6 Knight stopped both Ke5 and Ke6   4.Ke6  Nd4+ } 4... Kd2 5.
Bc4 Ke3 { The White pawns are helpless on dark squares } 6. Bg8 Kxf4 7. Bxh7
Kxg5
{ Shankland managed to clinch half a point from this game because he recognised that White's pawns were weak on dark squares, and all that he needed to do is activate the King. The Knight could have attacked the pawns but 2...Ne6 would be well met by 3. Ke5 }
*

[Event "?"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "5. Endgame Ideas"]
[Black "Puzzle #5"]
[Result "*"]
[FEN "1B4bk/7p/3Rp3/8/8/r7/4p3/4K3 w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]

1. Ra6
{ Our goal is to play Be5#. We can threaten it by moving our d6 Rook while attacking the Black Rook with either Ra6 or Rd3. Ra6 forces ...Re3 while Rd3 forces ...Ra5. Which is worse for Black?   1.Rd3  Ra5  Black's Rook has many squares along the fifth rank to keep an eye on the critical e5 square. Black will escape with ...Kg7 next }
1... Re3
{ Forcing the Black Rook to the very short e-file. Our next goal is to force the Rook of the e-file. There are only three squares we need to take care of, e3, e4, and e5. Since our King is glued to the e1 square, it is up to the rest of our pieces. How can our Rook and Bishop control a total of three squares? From the a-file, our Rook can control one square at a time, so it is up to the Bishop watch over the remaining two squares. For our Bishop to control two squares on the e-file, it will have to be on f4, where it controls e3 and e5. The final square, e4, is for our Rook to control. Now we have the final position in mind - our Bishop should be on f4 while our Rook should be on a4. But let's not forget that Black is just one move @@StartBracket@@...Kg7@@EndBracket@@ away from removing all mating ideas. This is why all our moves need to come with tempo }
2. Ra3
{ Forcing 2...Re4 so that we can we can play 3. Ra4 with tempo   2.Bf4??  Re4  3.Ra4  Rxf4  4.Rxf4 }
2... Re4 3. Ra4 Re3 4. Bf4
{ All the squares along the e-file are taken away. Black will either get mated or lose his Rook. }
*

