[White "5. Endgame Ideas"]
[Black "Illusion of Freedom"]
[Result "*"]
[FEN "3k4/7P/2PN4/8/8/1K6/8/b7 w - - 3 1"]
[SetUp "1"]

{ Sometimes, a long-ranged piece may seem quite free on an open board. However, upon further analysis, you may realise that every potential square it can go is somehow not available due to a myriad of  tactics  (such as a skewer or a fork)  . This is what I call the Illusion of Freedom. }
1. Ka2
{ For example, in this position, Black’s Bishop seems spoilt for choice with all the squares along the a1-h8 diagonal. However, as it turns out, no matter where the Bishop goes, the White Knight will eventually win it with a fork. Let’s explore the variations. }
1... Bh8
{ 1...Be5  2.Nf7+   1...Bc3  2.c7+  Kxc7  3.Nb5+   1...Bd4  2.c7+  Kxc7  3.Nb5+   1...Bf6  2.c7+  Kxc7  3.Ne8+   1...Bg7  2.c7+  Kxc7  3.Ne8+ }
2. Nf7+
{ Similar to this example, many Illusion of Freedom puzzles involve Knight forks and double attacks. Therefore, knowing the Knight well is winning half the battle against this quirky set of puzzles. And that is exactly what this section focuses - to help you become a Knight virtuoso.   A key part of understanding the Knight’s peculiar movement lies in recognising its interaction with colour complexes. On the most fundamental level, a Knight on a light square will control dark squares only. Conversely, a Knight on a dark square controls light squares only. Building on this observation, this means that two pieces are forkable by a Knight only if they are on the same colour complex. In this position, the Black King would either be on d8 or c7  (depending on whether we played c7+)  , both of which are dark squares. This was the reason Black’s dark-squared Bishop had no escape from the Knight. The Knight was on d6  (a dark square)  , ready to jump to the neighbouring light squares to fork the two Black pieces on dark squares.   As you work your way through the next few puzzles, we will see this concept in action in scenarios where the Knight needs to make more than two moves and where the Superposition Technique can be used. Keeping the colour complexes in mind, let’s see the Illusion of Freedom in action. }
*

[Event "?"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "5. Endgame Ideas"]
[Black "Puzzle #13"]
[Result "*"]
[FEN "1k6/3K1b2/8/8/1N6/8/4B3/8 w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]

1. Nc6+ { Forcing 1...Ka8 } 1... Ka8 { 1...Kb7  2.Nd8+ } 2. Bf3
{ Now White has a potent discovered attack and Black's Bishop is trapped. This is because White's Knight is on a light square, ready to move to a dark square. From there, it will give a discovered check while attacking the light square that the Black Bishop is on. }
2... Ba2
{ 2...Bg8  3.Ne7+   2...Bg6  3.Ne7+   2...Bc4  3.Na5+   2...Bb3  3.Na5+ } 3.
Nb4+ Ka7 4. Nxa2 *

[Event "?"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "5. Endgame Ideas"]
[Black "Puzzle #14"]
[Result "*"]
[FEN "5N2/N7/2P2p1p/2qp1kpP/8/3PP2P/8/3K4 w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]

1. c7 Qxc7 { Forced } 2. Nb5
{ To efficiently confirm if the Black Queen is trapped, we will need to use the Superposition Technique.  Wherever the Queen goes, we will have the option to play 3.Nd4+, forcing 3...Ke5. After that, we will have yet another option to play 4.Ng6+ which forces 4...Kd6. This means that the Black King is in a superposition of three squares - f5, e5, and d6. Whichever square allows a Knight fork, we will force the King there.  Bearing this in mind and returning to the current position, if the Queen moves to a light square, we should leave the King on f5, a light square, and look for a Knight fork.   If the Queen moves to a dark square, we should play 3.Nd4+, forcing the King to move to e5, a dark square. After 3...Ke5, we should look for a Knight fork. If the King and Queen still cannot be forked, all is not lost. We can play 4.Ng6+, forcing the Black King to g6, a different dark square, before looking for a Knight fork again.  Using the Superposition Technique in this way, working through all the variations will be a breeze. }
2... Qg7
{ 2...Qb6  3.Nd4+  Ke5  4.Nd7+   2...Qa5  3.Nd4+  Ke5  4.Nc6+   2...Qb7  3.Nd6+   2...Qb8  3.Nd4+  Ke5  4.Nc6+   2...Qd8  3.Nd4+  Ke5  4.Nc6+   2...Qg3  3.Nd4+  Ke5  4.Ng6+  Kd6  5.Nf5+   2...Qh2  3.Nd4+  Ke5  4.Nf3+   2...Qe7  3.Nd4+  Ke5  4.Ng6+   2...Qf7  3.Nd6+   2...Qc6  3.Nd4+   2...Qc5  3.Nd4+  Ke5  4.Nd7+ }
3. Nd4+ Ke5 4. Ng6+ Kd6 5. Nf5+ *

[Event "?"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "5. Endgame Ideas"]
[Black "Puzzle #15"]
[Result "*"]
[FEN "k7/pN6/8/3B1r2/5B1K/8/8/8 w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]

{ Black is forking both our Bishops. How should we deal with that? } 1. Bg2
Rxf4+ 2. Kg3
{ The Rook is trapped because the Knight singularly controls both light and dark squares.   If the Black Rook moves to a light square, the White Knight is ready to move away from b7 to create a discovered check while attacking the light square that the Rook is on  If the Black Rook moves to a dark square, the b7 Knight will move to a dark square and give a discovered check. Black will be forced to move his King to b8, a dark square. With both the Rook and King on a dark square, as well as our Knight ready to hop to a light square, we will have a Knight fork. }
2... Rf8
{ 2...Rf7  3.Nd6+  Kb8  4.Nxf7   2...Rf6  3.Nc5+  Kb8  4.Nd7+  Kc7  5.Nxf6   2...Rf5  3.Nd6+  Kb8  4.Nxf5   2...Rd4  3.Na5+  Kb8  4.Nc6+  Kc7  5.Nxd4   2...Rc4  3.Nd6+  Kb8  4.Nxc4   2...Rb4  3.Nc5+  Kb8  4.Na6+  Kc8  5.Nxb4   2...Ra4  3.Nc5+  Kb8  4.Nxa4 }
3. Nc5+
{ The Knight forces the King to b8, leaving the Rook and King in forking range }
3... Kb8 4. Nd7+ Kc8 5. Nxf8 *

