1. Qg7
{ Black's King is in an extremely precarious position. White has three excellently placed pawns and an active King participating in the attack. To start the attack, White must find a good way to unpin the g-pawn. The text move forks both of Black's pieces, forcing him to accept the sacrifice. }
1... Qxg7
{ Despite the serious material deficit, White's three-pawn attack will end the game.   1...Qa3  2.c3  Unpinning the g-pawn. White will play g4 next to finish Black off   1...Qe3  If Black is hell-bent on pinning the g-pawn, we can simply take the Rook and let the attack play itself  2.Qxh8  Kh6  3.Qf8+  Kh5  4.Qg7  h6  5.Qf6   1...e5  Again, White can always take the Rook and play against Black's pathetic King position.  2.Qxh8  Kh6  3.Qf8+  Kh5  4.Qg7  h6  5.Qxe5+  Qxe5  6.g4 }
2. g4+ Kh6 3. g5+ Kh5 4. g4#
{ After applying PIE, I hope that you can recognise how vulnerable the Black King on h5 in such a position. }
*

