[White "6. Typical Mistakes"]
[Black "Poor Visualisation"]
[Result "*"]

{ The final obstacle to clear in chess is poor visualisation. This requires mental muscles that cannot be strengthened by quick fixes alone. Instead, they need regular training. The best form of training that I recommend is playing blindfold chess. If you can easily play a 60-move game blindfolded, you will be able to calculate a 60-move combination error-free!  But don’t worry if you are unable to play a full game without the board at this stage. Here, I will introduce three blindfold chess exercises which should all be done without looking at the chessboard. Find something challenging yet manageable to be included in your training.   1. Attempt basic piece tours  The task here is to simply map a route for any piece to move from one square to another. For example, how would a Bishop move from g1 to f8? The solution would be g1-c5-f8. Needless to say, if the piece you selected is the Bishop, the start and end points must be of the same colour. I would recommend using either the Bishop or the Knight for this piece tour.  2. Complete the Knight's tour vs Queen  In this exercise, your Knight starts at the a1 square while the enemy has a Queen on e4. The objective is to move the Knight to every square that the Queen does not control without ever moving to a square the Queen attacks. After attempting this several times, you can change the Knight's starting square to get a fresh route.  3. Look at the board fourth move  Start a game of blindfold chess, briefly looking at the pieces on the board only at every fourth move. This trains your visualisation and memory while giving you the assurance that you will never go off course for more than four moves. Once this gets too comfortable, you can decrease the frequency at which you look at the game to every sixth or seventh move. Eventually, you will be ready to tackle the full game blindfolded. }
1. -- *

[Event "?"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "6. Typical Mistakes"]
[Black "Course Conclusion"]
[Result "*"]

{ Summarising the entire course, here is a guide to your chess calculation training:  WHILE SOLVING PUZZLES OR PLAYING GAMES:  1. Whenever you are solving puzzles, conscientiously execute the steps of the Burger Technique.  2. If you are playing long games, use the Burger Technique in sharp positions or when you smell blood.  3. For endgame positions, you may incorporate schematic thinking in step 2 of the Burger Technique as you deem fit.  4. For all positions, you may incorporate various forms of reciprocal thinking  (typical reciprocal thinking, identifying the King’s escape squares, and the Superposition Technique)  in step 3 of the Burger Technique as you deem fit.  WHILE STUDYING CHESS:  5. Whenever you find a new pattern, use the four steps of PIE to internalise it.  6. As you study theoretical endgames, always keep in mind how the positions can transpose among themselves. In this way, you are able to develop general endgame principles on your own.  7. Identify your most common calculation mistakes  (dismissing tactical motifs, unfamiliarity with patterns, and poor visualisation)  and work on them.  8. Train your visualisation with the blindfold chess exercises. }
1. -- *

